<node id="Insulina">
  <data key="d0">Insulina</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina is the peptide hormone naturally secreted by the β‑cells of the pancreas that plays a central role in maintaining normal blood‑glucose concentrations. By binding to insulin receptors on muscle, adipose and liver cells, insulina stimulates glucose uptake and hepatic glycogen synthesis, thereby lowering circulating glucose and preventing the hyperglycaemia and ketone production that occur when the hormone is deficient.

Because many individuals with type 1 diabetes and a substantial proportion of those with type 2 diabetes cannot generate sufficient endogenous insulin, insulina is manufactured as a medication for diabetes management. Therapeutically it is supplied exclusively for sub‑cutaneous administration and may be stored in refrigerated vials, cartridges, pre‑filled pens (e.g., KwikPen, BASAGLAR KwikPen, FlexPen, Novolin® R FlexPen) or in the reservoir of an insulin pump. In pump therapy the hormone is delivered through an infusion set, providing continuous basal delivery and discrete bolus doses for meals or correction; the reservoir must be replaced when infusion‑set adsorption or other delivery problems arise.

A wide spectrum of insulina formulations is marketed, differing in onset and duration of action. Short‑acting (regular) insulin covers post‑prandial glucose rises, while rapid‑acting analogues such as NovoRapid® and Fiasp® begin working within minutes. Intermediate‑acting preparations (e.g., NPH) extend the effect for several hours, and long‑acting analogues such as Tresiba provide basal insulin for up to 24 hours. Clinicians often combine these variants to match a patient’s metabolic needs, lifestyle and meal patterns, employing basal‑bolus dosing regimens that can be administered via pens, syringes or pumps.

Insulina must be stored refrigerated until use and should appear as a clear white‑to‑cloudy solution before injection. Because it markedly reduces blood glucose, excessive dosing, missed meals, improper injection technique, or pump delivery errors can precipitate hypoglycaemia. Patients are therefore instructed to monitor glucose levels closely, recognise symptoms of low glucose, adjust doses as needed, and follow proper storage and injection practices to ensure therapeutic effectiveness and minimise drug‑interaction risks. Dosage adjustments are also required during lactation to avoid adverse effects in nursing infants.

In Portuguese, insulina is described as “hormônio administrado para controlar a glicemia em pacientes diabéticos,” underscoring its universal role as the primary pharmacologic agent for controlling diabetes‑related blood‑glucose levels.</data>
