<node id="Hipoglicemia">
  <data key="d0">Hipoglicemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hipoglicemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally low blood‑glucose concentrations, most commonly defined as a plasma glucose level below 70 mg/dL (or &lt;3.9 mmol/L). The rapid decline in glucose can produce a spectrum of neurological and autonomic symptoms. Mild to moderate signs include tremor, cold sweats, pale skin, fatigue, confusion, palpitations, and impaired cognitive functions such as slower reaction time and reduced concentration, which may affect activities like driving. Severe manifestations can progress to seizures, loss of consciousness, and death. Even in the absence of overt symptoms, “silent” hypoglycemia can compromise safety, and nocturnal episodes are frequent and often more severe.

The condition most frequently arises as an adverse metabolic reaction to insulin therapy in people with diabetes. Hipoglicemia may occur when insulin is administered in excess, when dosing errors are made, or when insulin delivery is unintentionally accelerated. Specific procedural risk factors include intramuscular injection instead of sub‑cutaneous placement, sudden changes of injection site, alterations in insulin regimens, programming mistakes in insulin‑pump devices, and the use of rapid‑acting (e.g., Humalog) or long‑acting analogues (e.g., Tresiba, insulin glargine). Other glucose‑lowering medications, missed meals, and strenuous exercise can also precipitate low‑blood‑glucose episodes.

Epidemiological data from clinical trials indicate that hipoglicemia occurs in roughly 12 % of patients treated with Levemir®; about 6 % of those experience severe episodes that require third‑party assistance. Similar rates have been reported with products such as Novolin® R Penfill®. In trial reporting, hypoglycemic events are captured as episodes per patient per month, encompassing both symptomatic and silent cases, to provide a quantitative measure of frequency.

Management emphasizes regular glucose monitoring, typically with portable capillary blood‑glucose meters or continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems. CGM use has been shown to reduce the frequency of low‑blood‑glucose episodes, including during pregnancy. Preventive strategies focus on careful dose calculation, correct injection technique, appropriate device programming, and prompt therapy adjustments in response to changing glucose patterns. Immediate treatment with fast‑acting carbohydrates is recommended to restore glucose levels and alleviate symptoms. Consistent monitoring, disciplined dosing, and avoidance of rapid insulin absorption can substantially reduce the incidence and severity of hipoglicemia, influencing how treatment goals are set for individuals with diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia denotes low blood glucose levels, defined by thresholds such as &lt; 70 mg/dL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1be43b1b23852278983f60d0f150b0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d870035815524d8e4f6b0d6b05e5c2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b64741e11a7d852f462b1269fabfbe43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e490a7e963a20e2221a8097b50073543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2df359ca3783191b2159f8d3f6ea6c52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7982460136aa21cc2de3969af556c4d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aafda750d4900a464b1ac0c9bad5ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ce149c8d2ae4fe9b4a454053c8e0a71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a976e1642df27c488174759a35ca23f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-572e715aada476f4dac69a032e16cc09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c032d0271c85fd24982516368a62b1a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11cce86325de9748e794648198242578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da44554b006e6bd1b841f4246a054669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-072d97721eef045503ec1dce0363b58e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46c9571a4df1570d4a097dc949f512cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02780a8bc1d372cc47e0ccf2eaab1a99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a279eef99c9c70338c74ffc7b4e260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d0ac8ad5968d54922b924fdb16b61d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61cacf80ada6db26d2b975df763c137a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17844a4a12e6c05ba2d7b5f54b0f9bd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2db4ee43ed7cc39546184320e1328d06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b40c2fbad301807d9646f27d94635cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-518838d3c8e8110567b1891b704bfef9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef35bd45e4ba8d20f4e9181011ccfcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a247846b9ec5d44d0bcbe6c5f15599f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca0b919135813e1d5b772face36989d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cd336474a2e9457985162b1ddb4b603&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99841fd6d1e1fbf23f184e202bc760e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553e80da33ab48fb157ac9ff75436eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f859befb6230d94e8af9144794e0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56fb3d61e8c32af5d6b8be06cc54f904&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4ba52cc293c5c002b470566321026d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f46c503a378e6c69618036a038672417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2479751a6475c09415f0d79759db091d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29710f08f3e10bba4a464e8b4da1917c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f0b85829c02f82470489f3f5e487996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e212a20942ed5387efe96e92bdb4ba98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b060d4f68031bd35d