<node id="Regular Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Regular Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Regular Insulin is a short‑acting insulin that starts working in 30–60 minutes, peaks between 2–4 hours, and lasts up to 8 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular Insulin is a short‑acting insulin mentioned in the preparation steps.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular insulin is a short‑acting insulin formulation referenced when comparing dose changes with Humalog.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular Insulin is a short‑acting insulin used for prandial glucose control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd45744c4c50bb97abce1352018de47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Insulinoterapia – Diretriz da Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes – Ed. 2024.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786331421</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Pump">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Pump</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Pump is a small, wearable medical device that provides continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII) for people with diabetes whose pancreas does not produce enough insulin. The system typically comprises a disposable insulin reservoir, an infusion‑set (tubing and cannula), a display screen, programmable buttons, and a rechargeable battery; it can be worn on the waist, carried in a pocket, or affixed directly to the skin. The pump is designed to deliver rapid‑acting insulin analogs (e.g., Apidra Solostar, Fiasp) in a programmed basal pattern and in user‑initiated bolus doses, thereby mimicking normal pancreatic function, maintaining euglycemia, and reducing the need for multiple manual injections. Long‑acting insulins such as Levemir or regular insulins like Novolin R are contraindicated because they interfere with the delivery algorithm.

The device stores a full set of therapy parameters, including basal‑rate schedules, insulin‑to‑carbohydrate ratios, insulin‑sensitivity factors, and target glucose ranges. A built‑in “Bolus Wizard” calculates bolus amounts based on entered carbohydrate intake and current glucose levels. Users can run self‑tests, lock the pump, place it in storage mode, or manually suspend and later resume insulin delivery. Modern pumps integrate data from continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) sensors, allowing automatic insulin‑suspension when glucose approaches a low threshold (e.g., SmartGuard), real‑time dose adjustments, and alerts for out‑of‑range glucose, low‑reservoir levels, delivery errors, or other safety concerns. Visual, auditory, and vibration cues, as well as text messages, provide notifications and alarms.

Insulin Pump communicates wirelessly or via a meter with smartphones, tablets, or computers for data download, software updates, and remote monitoring. Although the hardware is manufactured waterproof, it can be affected by strong magnetic fields, radiation, extreme temperatures, electrostatic discharge, and electromagnetic interference; manufacturers therefore advise careful battery handling and storage, especially during travel. Pump failure can rapidly lead to hyperglycemia, ketosis, or diabetic ketoacidosis, so users are instructed to test blood‑ketone levels, follow troubleshooting steps, and seek technical support when needed.

The technology implements the continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (SICI) method and supports various bolus delivery patterns, including those required for rapid‑acting inhaled insulin such as Afrezza. Insulin Pump therapy is commonly paired with a CGM to enhance glycemic control and may display real‑time glucose data from the sensor on its screen. Representative commercial systems include the MiniMed™ 640G, 670G, and 780G platforms, which combine insulin delivery, CGM integration, automated safety features, and comprehensive alarm functionality. The device is considered appropriate for children, adolescents, and adults with diabetes and may be used by competent patients in inpatient settings under hospital guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;An insulin pump is a medical device that can display real‑time glucose data from tR‑SMCG to the user.</data>
