</node>
<node id="Bolus">
  <data key="d0">Bolus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bolus is a discrete, rapid‑acting insulin dose that is delivered either by an insulin pump or, for multiple‑daily‑injection regimens, as a single injection. It is administered in addition to the continuously supplied basal insulin to cover the carbohydrate content of a meal, to correct a rising blood‑glucose level, and, when needed, to treat or prevent impending hypoglycaemia after a missed dose. The dose is calculated for each occasion using the individual’s insulin‑to‑carbohydrate ratio and, when correction is required, a correction (or sensitivity) factor that estimates how many insulin units are needed to lower glucose by a specified amount. Devices such as the Accu‑Chek Solo provide a bolus‑calculation function that suggests the appropriate correction or meal dose based on entered glucose values, and pumps may also use sensor glucose readings to generate a bolus automatically (e.g., via SmartGuard).

Bolus delivery can be selected in several formats on modern pumps. A standard (instant) bolus releases the entire calculated amount immediately, while a square (extended) bolus spreads delivery over a user‑chosen interval to better match the absorption of large or high‑fat meals. Some systems offer a dual‑wave (combination of instant and extended) bolus. The user may enter the dose manually, choose it from a Bolus Wizard menu, or allow the pump’s algorithm to generate it automatically. While a bolus is in progress it can be interrupted by certain alarms (e.g., battery removal) and later resumed; it also temporarily prevents the SmartGuard feature from restarting until the bolus is completed.

In clinical practice each bolus is evaluated for adequacy by reviewing pre‑meal timing, post‑prandial glucose trends, and the correction of hyperglycaemia. These assessments inform adjustments to the insulin‑to‑carbohydrate ratio, correction factor, and pump settings. If a bolus is missed, it may be entered later with an adjusted timing to avoid hypoglycaemia. The bolus component is often expressed as a proportion of total daily insulin, reflecting its role as a rapid, time‑specific infusion that, together with basal insulin, constitutes intensive insulin therapy for managing post‑prandial glucose excursions and hyperglycaemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bolus is a rapid insulin dose administered before meals.</data>
