<node id="Insulina NPH">
  <data key="d0">Insulina NPH</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina NPH is an intermediate‑acting human insulin formulation that is supplied as a cloudy (leitoso) suspension, typically in 3 mL cartridges designed for use with a reusable insulin pen. Because it is a suspension, the product must be gently agitated before each injection to achieve proper resuspension, a step that directly influences its pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic profile. The insulin appears milky or white‑cloudy in the cartridge and is classified as a slow‑acting, intermediate‑duration agent that is often administered before meals and at bedtime.

In clinical practice and research, Insulina NPH is frequently employed as a comparator or reference treatment. It has been used as the standard insulin in studies evaluating retinopathy outcomes, treatment satisfaction, and safety versus newer basal analogues such as insulin glargine (e.g., in gestational and pre‑existing diabetes cohorts). The formulation has also served as the comparator for onset‑of‑action assessments and for evaluating the efficacy of insulin glargine in various trial settings, including Study J where it was added once daily at bedtime.

Insulina NPH can be mixed with rapid‑acting insulin analogues such as Humalog, but only under medical supervision and according to specific mixing and administration procedures. Patients previously managed with NPH‑based regimens may be switched to newer basal options such as the BASAGLAR KWIKPEN, reflecting ongoing transitions in diabetes management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate‑acting insulin used as comparator in the clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;NPH insulin is a short‑acting basal insulin used as a reference for comparing hypoglycemia risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina NPH is an intermediate‑acting insulin used as a comparator for hypoglycaemia risk and weight‑gain profile with Levemir®.&lt;SEP&gt;Neutral protamine Hagedorn intermediate‑acting insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate‑acting NPH insulin, referenced as a comparator to ultra‑rapid analogues.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina NPH is NPH insulin administered at bedtime to lower the risk of nocturnal hypoglycemia.</data>
