<node id="Lipohypertrophy">
  <data key="d0">Lipohypertrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipohypertrophy is a localized thickening or enlargement of subcutaneous fatty tissue that occurs at insulin injection sites in people with diabetes. The condition develops when insulin is repeatedly administered into the same area without adequate site rotation, or when improper needle technique is used, leading to the formation of hardened nodules, lumps, or swelling within the skin’s fatty layer. It has been reported in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes and can affect patients of any age, including young individuals who require frequent insulin administration.

The presence of lipohypertrophy can disrupt normal insulin absorption, resulting in irregular pharmacokinetics and potentially compromising glycaemic control. Because the altered tissue absorbs insulin more slowly or unpredictably, patients may experience unexpected hyperglycaemia or require higher insulin doses. The condition is listed as an adverse reaction associated with specific insulin products, such as BASAGLAR (used primarily for type 1 diabetes) and Lantus® (used for type 2 diabetes), and it may also be observed in users of insulin pumps, although some sources acknowledge it only briefly without extensive documentation.

Prevention and management of lipohypertrophy rely on systematic rotation of injection sites, using proper needle length and technique, and avoiding repeated injections into the same spot. Regular inspection of injection areas and palpation for lumps can help identify early changes, allowing patients and clinicians to adjust injection practices and reduce the risk of further tissue thickening. When lipohypertrophy is detected, rotating away from the affected area and, if necessary, consulting a healthcare professional for guidance on site selection and insulin dosing are recommended to restore optimal insulin absorption and maintain stable blood‑glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohypertrophy is an abnormal increase in fat tissue associated with NRTI use and insulin resistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e480a6a15b0d8e7069746e3f3f788f80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c580ebfc111aa29796af1a6f15941c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8934881ec5ab7cfeb836126b3f0e8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779af610d7298ce98490962cd90b4e76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8644d98286c4a296063176435b45d589&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3b81193a603a098b1ff9f6f0f665fec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00d07129873894814fe6540ce243ef9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55e58732610596ce9a8f3145d34f75a7</data>
