</node>
<node id="Insulin Pen">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Pen</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Pen is a modern, pen‑style device designed for the self‑administration of insulin by subcutaneous injection. The device combines a cartridge of insulin—either pre‑filled or refillable—with a short, disposable needle that must be fully inserted before activation. A dose‑setting mechanism, such as a turning dial or a scale, allows the user to select an exact amount of insulin, which is then delivered by pressing an injector button. Many models also incorporate a dose counter that displays the remaining units, supporting accurate dosing and tight glycemic control.

The reusable body of the insulin pen eliminates the need to draw insulin into syringes, simplifying the injection process and reducing preparation time. Its compact shape and disposable needle contribute to comfort, lower the risk of needle‑related complications, and make multiple daily injections practical and fast. The pen is compatible with various insulin formulations, including long‑acting products such as Lantus, and can be used with cartridges specifically prepared for the device.

Guidelines and research emphasize that the insulin pen should be stored at recommended ambient temperatures rather than in a refrigerator, because exposure to cold can impair the pen’s internal mechanism and affect insulin potency. Studies on temperature effects underline the importance of maintaining proper storage conditions to preserve both device integrity and drug stability.

Since its introduction, the insulin pen has become a widely accepted alternative to traditional syringes, praised by patients and healthcare providers for its convenience, reliability, and ability to improve adherence to insulin therapy regimens. Its practical design and precise dosing capabilities have contributed to broader acceptance and routine use in diabetes management worldwide.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin Pen is a portable device for delivering insulin, serving as an alternative to pump infusion.&lt;SEP&gt;Practical, quick, easy‑to‑use device for multiple daily insulin injections.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin pens are devices that enable more accurate and less painful insulin dosing.&lt;SEP&gt;A disposable device for delivering insulin, compared with traditional vial/syringe delivery methods.&lt;SEP&gt;A traditional handheld device for insulin injection, used as a comparator in an insulin delivery trial.</data>
