<node id="Diabetes Care">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Care</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Care is a peer‑reviewed, high‑impact medical and scientific journal that serves as a primary venue for publishing research, guidelines, and reviews on every aspect of diabetes. It covers diagnosis, classification, prevention, management, treatment, and complications, and is widely read by researchers, clinicians, and policymakers worldwide in both print and online formats.

The journal regularly features results from clinical trials and pharmacokinetic investigations of insulin products, including NPH insulin, insulin detemir, insulin glargine, and inhaled‑insulin studies such as the INHALE‑1 and INHALE‑3 trials. Notable publications include the 2015 NPH insulin resuspension analysis, the Onset 7 Trial, the COMISAIR Study, the LEADER trial analysis, and numerous randomized comparative studies in type 1 and type 2 diabetes. Diabetes Care also publishes research on automated insulin‑delivery systems, continuous and flash glucose monitoring, insulin‑pump technology, and the use of insulin pens in elderly patients, illustrating its broad technological scope.

Guidance on glycemic targets and standards of care is a core focus; the journal has issued articles such as “Glycemic Targets: Standards of Care in Diabetes 2023” and disseminates widely cited studies on hemoglobin A1c, mortality, hypoglycaemia, cognitive function, and overall glycaemic control. It addresses diabetes‑related complications through comprehensive reviews of lipohypertrophy, ketoacidosis, autoantibodies in type 1 diabetes risk, and ketosis‑prone and type 3c diabetes. Large‑scale investigations on screening cost‑effectiveness, prediction of type 1 diabetes, and gestational diabetes outcomes also appear regularly.

Diabetes Care has published key classification and diagnostic resources, including a prospective population‑based study that used clinical and C‑peptide criteria, and a 2023 supplement dedicated to the “Classification and Diagnosis of Diabetes” article. The journal frequently features reports and consensus statements from the International Expert Committee and the American Diabetes Association, as well as studies on lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions for diabetes prevention.

Rigorous peer review ensures that both clinical and experimental findings meet the high standards expected by the diabetes research community. Its extensive catalogue of cited articles, figures, and supplements underscores its central role in shaping contemporary diabetes research, practice, and policy.</data>
