<node id="Diabetologia">
  <data key="d0">Diabetologia</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetologia is a peer‑reviewed scientific journal dedicated to the full spectrum of diabetes research. It publishes original investigations, reviews, meta‑analyses, consensus reports, and abstracts from major conferences, targeting clinicians, investigators, policymakers, and other stakeholders who need up‑to‑date evidence on diabetes mellitus, its complications and treatment strategies. The journal accepts a wide array of study designs, including randomized controlled trials, observational studies, large‑cohort analyses, and mechanistic laboratory work, making it a central platform for both clinical and translational diabetes science.

The publication record of Diabetologia shows a strong emphasis on pharmacological advances. Early work such as a 1988 study examined how storage temperature affects insulin pharmacokinetics. Subsequent comparative trials have evaluated insulin glargine versus NPH insulin, insulin detemir versus insulin glargine (cited in a 2008 trial), and the efficacy of insulin analogues compared with human insulins in basal‑bolus regimens. A meta‑analysis of short‑acting insulin analogues and research on the suppression of endogenous glucose production further illustrate the journal’s focus on insulin innovation. In 2009, Diabetologia published a pivotal intensive‑glucose‑control trial that linked tight glycaemic management to macrovascular outcomes in type 2 diabetes, and it has also featured studies relating glycated hemoglobin levels to microvascular complications and assessing 1‑hour oral glucose tolerance test values as predictors of disease risk.

Beyond drug therapy, the journal disseminates broader advances in diabetes care. A 2023 consensus report on automated insulin‑delivery systems and numerous conference abstracts keep readers abreast of emerging technologies. Observational research on flash glucose monitoring in large adult cohorts exemplifies its commitment to population‑level insights. Diabetologia has also published articles on hypoglycaemia in type 1 diabetes (2018), on latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA), and on prediction models for maturity‑onset diabetes of the young (MODY).

Overall, Diabetologia serves as an authoritative, comprehensive source for diabetes research, covering molecular mechanisms, therapeutic evaluations, epidemiological outcomes, and cutting‑edge technologies, thereby supporting a broad international audience of researchers, healthcare professionals, and health‑policy decision‑makers.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a journal referenced for a study on severe insulin deficiency in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia, a peer‑reviewed journal publishing data on SGLT2 inhibitors and diabetic ketoacidosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a peer‑reviewed journal focusing on diabetes research.&lt;SEP&gt;A journal publishing diabetes research.</data>
