<node id="Lipohipertrofia">
  <data key="d0">Lipohipertrofia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipohipertrofia is a localized overgrowth or thickening of subcutaneous fatty tissue that occurs at injection sites, most commonly in patients receiving insulin therapy. It is classified as a specific form of lipodystrophy in which the subcutaneous fat becomes noticeably enlarged and firm. The condition typically arises when injections are repeatedly administered in the same area, especially if proper site rotation is not practiced or if needles are reused, leading to repeated trauma of the local tissue.

Clinical observations note that lipohipertrofia appears in approximately 1 %–2 % of patients treated with insulin glargine, although the exact incidence may vary with different insulin preparations and injection techniques. Some regulatory and pharmacovigilance sources list lipohipertrofia as an adverse reaction associated with insulin use, while other documentation separates it from the formal adverse‑reaction tables, treating it instead as a tissue‑overgrowth phenomenon that does not meet the typical criteria for an adverse drug event. Both perspectives acknowledge the same underlying manifestation—a localized fatty swelling—but differ on its categorization within safety reporting frameworks.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohipertrofia is a subtype of lipodistrofia characterized by abnormal fat enlargement.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohyperplasia is skin thickening that can result from lipodystrophy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b4dfd5f7bda9f876ccb805f6f77f34e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c032d0271c85fd24982516368a62b1a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11cce86325de9748e794648198242578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da44554b006e6bd1b841f4246a054669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d194d06ef99b3f8aa6b6542b7e93e762&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-072d97721eef045503ec1dce0363b58e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e50c145614f04be2319a31b74b80411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-922791f49d1bf928fe4f02ddd336927b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7ca732691e1cbcafd65e378b8d80144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-929e31613caadc967412041c8c2155b3</data>
