<node id="HbA1c">
  <data key="d0">HbA1c</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">HbA1c (Hemoglobin A1c, also called glycated or glycosylated hemoglobin) is a laboratory measurement that quantifies the proportion of hemoglobin molecules whose β‑chains have become irreversibly bound to glucose. Because erythrocytes circulate for approximately 120 days, the HbA1c value reflects average blood‑glucose exposure over the preceding two to three months. Results are most often reported as a percentage (e.g., 6.5 %) and may also be expressed in mmol/mol units.

In clinical practice HbA1c is the principal biomarker for diagnosing diabetes mellitus and for screening the disease. Most guidelines set a diagnostic threshold of ≥ 6 % (or ≥ 6.5 % in many recommendations); values above 8.6 % are sometimes cited in specialized criteria, such as those for latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA) or for distinguishing certain diabetes subtypes. Normal values for non‑pregnant adults are generally &lt; 5.7 %. Therapeutic targets vary by population: a general goal of &lt; 7 % is widely endorsed, while stricter targets are advised for women who are pregnant or planning pregnancy (≤ 6.5 % pre‑conception and first trimester, ≤ 6 % later trimesters). Professional societies differ slightly (e.g., NICE ≤ 6.5 %, ISPAD ≤ 7 %, ADA ≤ 7.5 %). Some studies propose an optimal early‑pregnancy cutoff around 5.9 % for detecting gestational diabetes risk.

HbA1c is also the dominant efficacy endpoint in diabetes clinical trials and is routinely used to monitor long‑term glycaemic control across therapeutic strategies. Baseline HbA1c values in insulin‑ and drug‑evaluation studies frequently average ≈ 8.6 %, with end‑of‑study means near 6.9 % for agents such as insulin glargine, NPH insulin, and pediatric formulations like Toujeo 300 U/mL. The metric enables head‑to‑head comparisons of products (e.g., Levemir® vs. NPH) and of delivery devices. Interventions that improve glucose monitoring—continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) or structured self‑monitoring of blood glucose—typically produce modest HbA1c reductions, reflecting improved time‑in‑range, although occasional trials show unchanged HbA1c despite other therapeutic gains, underscoring its stability as an average‑glucose indicator.

Despite its strengths, HbA1c does not capture short‑term glucose spikes, post‑prandial excursions, or hypoglycaemic episodes, and therefore it is often complemented by CGM or finger‑stick data to assess glycaemic variability. Pediatric registry analyses indicate that HbA1c levels are not correlated with severe hypoglycaemia rates, highlighting a limitation for risk stratification. Accuracy can be compromised by conditions that alter hemoglobin composition or red‑cell lifespan, such as hemoglobinopathies, iron‑deficiency anemia, chronic kidney disease, and glucocorticoid‑induced hyperglycaemia.

Overall, HbA1c remains a central, objective biomarker for diabetes diagnosis, therapeutic target setting, and outcome measurement in guidelines and research, while its recognized limitations mandate adjunctive glucose monitoring for comprehensive diabetes management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8d870035815524d8e4f6b0d6b05e5c2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b64741e11a7d852f462b1269fabfbe43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8375bd3e65b9aa9965acd81fd9a448f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6828aacfba0c2a6ae59c439a4e4b161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74b3171b97fd80965359afadc0af9312&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4f2935f492529f0d551140b53a84fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecd2466ef4258dade38fbadd495188e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe094dd3d94334373a8497a7cb841e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89eaf7a8bcccf1ef8657b22ee03b5dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afc9722167670b0bfeef0b43f4b0df79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c078e8c2861146fb455f1dc3d4af2456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-072d97721eef045503ec1dce0363b58e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72472938feb27131681a1591081e8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b916f29e047f8579110cf5da3fd73847&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d648fb4d9a20040dd617a7ca164c9f10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a592aedb257bad9532268b4056ec15c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce5998d1ff6a67e2e549405b16f744f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f290894e2d3b4216cac1721f22bb67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d9c944d51a42525c017d9109f46684c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-863286c185f2a62e5b28d7e4577a1088&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553e80da33ab48fb157ac9ff75436eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76df79998cbb923bc6c94e375c220eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-228b9514d97e62787d4a60078f932ceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78b302b74d715df9c3b9e9db38fa4c64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18f8ab53481bf1b63fda1bb71fe80ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecb3968c37f57b78a756e4b661ad089&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-891989adf06518cd34c94853444495e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f85d0ab4cfadc905544cb9e30752dab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff211ed5ca723fa2074871af58a4eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77c1608c27e3114f3a14f175efe260a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2073bddd90c7dd8e156b67821f0f3247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7ca48868f79f7be782198d67ffefd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7e36686d077dcb6239405f60ea031&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a4f77ab697e068f8cf31533065722b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e7e47ca39ac4f4975da2b4b7e6d3c36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68b98187c6ab15468f1c0d8f5e7f9f6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767d025776f0f59c0