</node>
<node id="Skin Fold (Prega Cutânea)">
  <data key="d0">Skin Fold (Prega Cutânea)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Skin fold (prega cutânea) is a technique used to raise subcutaneous tissue before insulin injection to avoid intramuscular delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-26c5ab6d7b3b32c5f6aca2b58489782a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786298845</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="45° Injection Angle">
  <data key="d0">45° Injection Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">45° injection angle is recommended for insulin administration in children, adolescents, or adults with low subcutaneous tissue to prevent intramuscular injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-26c5ab6d7b3b32c5f6aca2b58489782a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786298845</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="90° Injection Angle">
  <data key="d0">90° Injection Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">90° injection angle is the standard angle for insulin subcutaneous injection in most adults and children.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-26c5ab6d7b3b32c5f6aca2b58489782a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786298845</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subcutaneous Tissue">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous Tissue</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Subcutaneous tissue is the layer of fat under the skin where insulin is intended to be injected.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous Tissue is the fatty layer just below the skin where insulin is normally injected for gradual absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The tissue layer under the dermis where insulin is injected; its thickness can vary widely within the same individual.&lt;SEP&gt;The layer of tissue beneath the skin where insulin is deposited to avoid intramuscular injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous tissue is the layer beneath the skin where the needle is inserted and insulin is delivered.&lt;SEP&gt;The layer of tissue beneath the skin where insulin is injected.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous tissue is the layer of fat under the skin where insulin is administered subcutaneously.</data>
