<node id="Glicemia">
  <data key="d0">Glicemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Glicemia is the concentration of glucose present in the bloodstream, typically expressed in milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL). It is measured either through laboratory analysis or by point‑of‑care devices such as self‑testing glucometers and continuous glucose sensors (including those used by the American Medical Group Companies, AMGC). As a direct indicator of blood glucose level, glicemia provides a quantitative assessment of an individual’s metabolic status.

In clinical practice, glicemia is a central metric for the diagnosis and management of diabetes mellitus. Patients, especially those on insulin or other antihyperglycemic agents, are instructed to monitor their glicemia regularly to keep glucose values within prescribed target ranges and to detect episodes of hyperglycemia or hypoglycemia promptly. Common safety thresholds are cited—for example, values below 70 mg/dL may signal hypoglycemia, while values above 90 mg/dL may prompt therapeutic adjustments. These thresholds guide clinicians and patients in making immediate safety decisions and long‑term treatment plans.

Beyond diagnosis, glicemia serves as a therapeutic monitoring tool. Changes in glicemia are used to evaluate the effectiveness of pharmacologic interventions; for instance, long‑acting insulin analogs such as Tresiba® (insulin degludec) are noted for their ability to lower glicemia and improve overall glycemic control. Regular glicemia measurements allow healthcare providers to assess how well insulin regimens or other medications are working, to fine‑tune dosages, and to reduce the risk of complications associated with sustained elevated blood glucose. Consequently, glicemia integrates diagnostic, monitoring, and therapeutic decision‑making functions in the management of metabolic health.&lt;SEP&gt;Glicemia refers to blood glucose concentration, with thresholds (&gt;100 mg/dL preferred, &gt;200 mg/dL) mentioned for Peptídeo C testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a976e1642df27c488174759a35ca23f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a247846b9ec5d44d0bcbe6c5f15599f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1aba4eb027a027fb1ad6e0105b97cfa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cd336474a2e9457985162b1ddb4b603&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1124e7acf6619a1a7c60febd5211c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0689fd73d2061ee466b9b90e03bda141&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93f7f04b0529d6f0ace11829cc4fa3b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60530d1861f7c2d1efecc069c0913d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79d0e4d0f656bd87d2d0c4657378db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5341c88e6470a893413f9aaec70dbdae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a0699569d4d9d7344609e3680eec66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f645bd7019e94151caaff47d6cbfcaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e0d8e22a06c73855abed11561ce467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dce7a759a9de18a4d1bcc77a071fbe2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e6267110f4ae5f4df4c49c0653a09b2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/HIPO_HIPER.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Orientacoes_Hipoglicemia_SBD_transcricao.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786372998</data>
