<node id="Associação Americana de Diabetes (ADA)">
  <data key="d0">Associação Americana de Diabetes (ADA)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Associação Americana de Diabetes (ADA) is a professional organization that issues recommendations for insulin injection site rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alcohol 70%">
  <data key="d0">Alcohol 70%</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Alcohol 70% is a topical disinfectant composed of a 70 % alcohol solution, most commonly isopropyl alcohol. It is routinely employed to cleanse the skin at the injection site prior to insulin administration, ensuring a sterile surface for subcutaneous injection. In addition to skin preparation, the solution is used to clean insulin‑related equipment, including insulin vials, vial caps, and insulin pens, thereby reducing the risk of microbial contamination during the injection process.

The use of Alcohol 70% is recommended in a variety of clinical and community settings where insulin is administered, such as health posts, schools, nursing homes, and other healthcare facilities. Its antiseptic properties make it an essential component of safe insulin injection protocols, helping to maintain hygiene and prevent infection for both patients and caregivers.&lt;SEP&gt;70 % alcohol solution applied for disinfection of insulin vial caps and the skin before injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Alcohol 70% is a disinfectant solution used to cleanse the skin before insulin injection to reduce infection risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A 70 % alcohol solution used to clean the skin before insulin injection in institutional settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Alcohol 70% is a disinfectant used to aseptically prepare the injection site and equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;A 70 % alcohol solution used for aseptic disinfection of the rubber stopper and injection site.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8768fa32f88d682aa4ddf677297047e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8310c065da1c624d58d9a0a8e9ff7d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e150bb4043de862453b3e96cf6cb663e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5de4e99a192602fe98c8b7e8e270a49c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301819</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vial">
  <data key="d0">Vial</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Vial is a glass container that holds insulin for loading into a syringe before injection.&lt;SEP&gt;The vial is a glass container that stores insulin before it is drawn into a syringe.&lt;SEP&gt;A vial is referenced as a container that should not be mixed with Toujeo 300 U/mL.&lt;SEP&gt;Vial is a container for insulin medication used as a backup for pump users.&lt;SEP&gt;Vial is a container of rapid‑acting insulin prescribed by a medical professional.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee5400065df79b02b6c04efc0f4854d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1700b8f7c792afbd394001e28bf632f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6737e479a665e986547e5e1e29ef26da</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786324171</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Site">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection Site refers to the specific area of skin on the body where insulin is administered. It is the location that is prepared with an antiseptic such as alcohol before a needle is inserted, and it is typically chosen for sub‑cutaneous delivery of the medication. Common injection sites include the abdomen, upper arm, thigh, and buttocks, each providing a convenient skin fold that can be lifted to allow proper needle placement.

The selected injection site influences how quickly insulin is absorbed into the bloodstream, which can affect the drug’s onset of action, overall effectiveness, and the required dose. For example, when Humalog (insulin lispro) is injected, variations in site selection may lead to differences in absorption rates and therapeutic outcome. Proper aseptic technique and consistent site rotation are therefore recommended to optimize insulin delivery and maintain consistent glycemic control.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection Site refers to the specific anatomical location where a subcutaneous injection is administered, influencing drug absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection site refers to the skin area where insulin is administered, with changes potentially causing delayed absorption and hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection site denotes the subcutaneous area where insulin is administered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb10c00517fa772be2ca9a42f497edf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b9c41c968bcc1d46185583661e8f989&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca13e396b094f0c2f482892f220f538&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf25fca295be60e28b7c7d3103f722fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d10a1f849386df540104c79e33a7b242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1245bd9ff75c998b49dab861cc4cebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319694</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quadrant">
  <data key="d0">Quadrant</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A quadrant is a sub‑area within an injection site used for rotating injection points.&lt;SEP&gt;Subdivision of a larger injection area (e.g., abdomen) used to organize rotation of insulin sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Quadrant is a sub‑area division of an injection site, used to organize systematic rotation of insulin injections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300289</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arm">
  <data key="d0">Arm</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The arm is a body part commonly used as an injection site for insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper limb area commonly used as an insulin injection site.&lt;SEP&gt;A common site on the body where continuous glucose sensors may be attached.&lt;SEP&gt;Common region of the body used for subcutaneous insulin injections.&lt;SEP&gt;Arm is a body site that can be used for insulin injection, typically assigned to a specific time of day in a rotation schedule.&lt;SEP&gt;Arm is a body region where insulin injection yields relatively fast absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4902fb11e4844af5cf6fd61f18b6e4ce</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301837</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leg">
  <data key="d0">Leg</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The leg is a body part that can be used as an injection site for insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Lower limb area used for insulin injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Common region of the body used for subcutaneous insulin injections.&lt;SEP&gt;Leg is a body site that can be used for insulin injection, typically assigned to a specific time of day in a rotation schedule.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300288</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Angle">
  <data key="d0">Injection Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection angle is the degree at which the needle enters the skin, influencing whether insulin reaches subcutaneous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection angle refers to the degree (45° or 90°) at which the syringe needle is inserted into tissue during insulin administration.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection Angle refers to the recommended degree (90°, 45°‑90°, or 45°) at which a needle is inserted based on needle length and patient age.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle at which the needle is introduced, oriented by the health professional during subcutaneous injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301073</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle Length">
  <data key="d0">Needle Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle length categories (4‑5 mm, 6‑8 mm, &gt;8 mm) determine the appropriate injection angle for adults and children.&lt;SEP&gt;The measured length of the injection needle (e.g., 4 mm, 5 mm, 6 mm, 8 mm, &gt;8 mm) that determines the appropriate application angle.&lt;SEP&gt;The length of a needle, ranging from 4 to 12.7 mm, influencing injection pain and risk of intramuscular injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786335927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pregnancy is the physiological condition of carrying a developing embryo or fetus, a gestational state that brings extensive metabolic and hormonal changes affecting maternal health and fetal development. Throughout the three trimesters, insulin requirements may rise, fall, or fluctuate, so women with diabetes need frequent dose adjustments and close monitoring of glycaemic control to protect both mother and child. Because pregnancy alters red‑cell turnover and plasma volume, HbA1c results can be less reliable, prompting clinicians to rely on more frequent capillary glucose testing and to set specific glycaemic targets for the gestational period.

In the context of insulin therapy, pregnancy modifies recommended injection practices. The abdomen, a common sub‑cutaneous site, should be avoided in the last trimester to reduce the risk of fetal exposure, and alternative sites such as the thigh or upper arm are preferred. Overall insulin pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics may change, requiring individualized dosing and vigilant observation. Clinical guidelines provide distinct glycaemic goals for pregnant patients, emphasizing tight glucose control while balancing the risk of hypoglycaemia.

Evidence for many modern insulin formulations in pregnancy remains limited. No well‑controlled studies have evaluated the safety or PK/PD profile of Toujeo® (insulin glargine U‑300) in pregnant individuals, and basal insulins such as Tresiba® (insulin degludec) and standard insulin glargine lack systematic investigation, leaving their safety uncertain. Data on the rapid‑acting analogue APIDRA® (insulin glulisine) are similarly sparse; neither the standard formulation nor the SOLOSTAR® delivery system has been studied in gestation, and although post‑marketing experience has not identified safety signals, APIDRA is not recommended without explicit medical guidance. Information on Fiasp® (fast‑acting insulin aspart) is also limited, and dosage adjustments are advised when it is prescribed during pregnancy, always under professional supervision.

Consequently, pregnancy complicates diabetes management by altering insulin needs, injection techniques, and the reliability of traditional monitoring tools. Clinicians must base treatment decisions on careful clinical judgment, individualized therapy plans, and vigilant monitoring of maternal and fetal outcomes, recognizing that the evidence base for many insulin products—including Toujeo, Tresiba, insulin glargine, APIDRA, and Fiasp—is still emerging.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnancy (second/third trimester and postpartum) can alter the relationship between A1C and glycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnancy is a physiological condition that can independently affect hemoglobin A1c measurements.&lt;SEP&gt;The physiological condition of carrying a developing fetus, discussed in relation to diabetes screening and outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b14c2f0985ae4a417c8fc99e339bd9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0f18c80f8de2dee960a03599d12052&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f14ce019f10c2a88765380143498ba5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-133169995b3ab942fd96f1fdfe7269ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4319d08c10ed2b48857a6d42eadd9db0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3397508bdfddde019cb1dfdcfb352435&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d0e34bef478ed432c8de7ef68dc9025&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c95471c4c993fa3a499f99443ee7c922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2684edaea64aa597fd29c3bbda611d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50719bcc88f4515ec825b9d5a7aba107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d10a1f849386df540104c79e33a7b242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c32255a7f064c18ff0b079798cfeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767d025776f0f59c05788e25616f412c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e136948d5572acf031988fd092dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00f6c2252dc945e977b3a2b2f495ffe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4b76ae88b212b3a0a4d05c250e05e0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066ba9218f6293a2f3dc5459d69239cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2cd796639bdb0e10eeda7ef3d9ebbce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b275f50028ef32e322bf8643e0342810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44888abd4ca8a1953f751ed6c41922e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc61bc2e95346d553972b4371912e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1215318cea8c61dc12042476611f9f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/metas cuidado do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377222</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rotation Plan">
  <data key="d0">Rotation Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A rotation plan is a schedule that alternates injection sites and quadrants to allow healing and avoid skin damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Healing Time">
  <data key="d0">Healing Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Healing time refers to the 14‑day period required before re‑using the same injection point to allow skin recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Skin Lesion">
  <data key="d0">Skin Lesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A skin lesion is a nodule or injury that can develop from repeated injections at the same spot.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin lesion is an injury or abnormality on the skin that can develop at insulin injection sites, such as lipohypertrophy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300289</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin absorption is the process by which injected insulin enters the bloodstream, affected by technique and site rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Control">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Control</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes control is the management of blood‑glucose levels, improved by proper insulin administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pain and Discomfort">
  <data key="d0">Pain and Discomfort</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pain and discomfort are adverse sensations that can be reduced by correct injection technique and site rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mixing Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Mixing Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mixing insulin refers to combining rapid‑acting and intermediate‑acting insulin in a single syringe, requiring a fixed‑needle syringe and correct order.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixing Insulin is the practice of combining rapid‑acting and intermediate‑acting insulin in the same syringe in a specific order.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300283</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Technique">
  <data key="d0">Injection Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection Technique is the comprehensive, standardized method used to prepare and administer insulin subcutaneously, ensuring optimal absorption and safety. It encompasses every step from pre‑injection hygiene—such as hand washing, aseptic site cleaning, and creation of a skin fold when appropriate—to the precise mechanics of needle insertion and post‑injection management.

The technique specifies the angle and depth at which the needle should be inserted, the dwell time required for proper insulin delivery, and the handling of the injection site, including whether a fold is needed for thinner skin. It also defines post‑injection care, such as monitoring for leakage, applying gentle pressure, and disposing of the needle according to medical‑waste protocols. These elements are designed to minimize tissue trauma, reduce pain, and promote consistent pharmacokinetic profiles.

Guidelines from professional bodies, notably the American Association of Diabetes Educators (AADE), formalize these practices and are routinely taught by doctors, nurses, and diabetes educators. The recommendations apply to a variety of delivery devices, including insulin pens such as the Caneta SoloStar, and are reinforced by healthcare professionals who tailor the procedure to individual patient needs and injection sites.

Overall, Injection Technique represents the collectively endorsed procedural framework for delivering insulin doses safely and effectively, integrating preparation, needle handling, site management, and disposal as advised by qualified medical personnel.&lt;SEP&gt;Injection Technique refers to the proper methods for administering insulin subcutaneously, intramuscularly, or intravenously.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-565255f3b3e7de3b17f81233ab493d9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb10c00517fa772be2ca9a42f497edf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca13e396b094f0c2f482892f220f538&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7223c781e745e72311fccfcdd58583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb8954aaa1e1230598596ce6f5df249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c24a2af0d0291fc4f4730256c420e93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ac99e7359716e2cecc2ec93159218b6</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-20201.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319740</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dwell Time">
  <data key="d0">Dwell Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dwell Time is the period the needle remains in the skin after injection, typically 5–10 seconds, to ensure full insulin delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Dwell Time refers to the period the needle remains inserted in the skin (5 seconds for syringe, 10 seconds for pen) to ensure full insulin delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300285</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed‑Needle Syringe">
  <data key="d0">Fixed‑Needle Syringe</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Fixed‑Needle Syringe is a recommended insulin injection syringe with a permanently attached needle, considered the safest option.&lt;SEP&gt;A fixed‑needle syringe is a syringe with a permanently attached needle, required for safely mixing two types of insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299817</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle Protector">
  <data key="d0">Needle Protector</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A needle protector is a safety device used to cover the needle after injection to prevent accidental sticks.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle Protector is a safety cap used to cover a needle after removal to prevent accidental sticks.&lt;SEP&gt;Protective cap that covers the insulin needle before and after injection to maintain sterility.&lt;SEP&gt;The protector that covers the needle during preparation and before injection.&lt;SEP&gt;The needle protector is rotated to loosen and remove the needle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8310c065da1c624d58d9a0a8e9ff7d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdabdf4730f349f4b46e7242c65d440d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786325536</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disposal Container">
  <data key="d0">Disposal Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A disposal container is a designated receptacle for discarding used syringes and needles safely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299817</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subcutaneous Fold">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous Fold</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Subcutaneous fold (also called prega subcutânea or skin‑pinch technique) is a simple manual method used immediately before inserting a needle for insulin administration. The procedure involves using the thumb and index finger to grasp a small amount of skin and lift it away from the underlying muscle, creating a temporary “fold” of subcutaneous tissue.  

By raising this layer of fatty tissue, the practitioner ensures that the needle penetrates only the subcutaneous compartment, thereby reducing the risk of inadvertent intramuscular injection. This technique is especially recommended for patients who require precise insulin dosing, as delivery into muscle can alter absorption rates and affect glycemic control.  

The pinch is performed at the moment of needle insertion, and the fold is maintained until the insulin has been fully injected. The resulting skin fold provides a clear visual and tactile cue that the injection will be placed within the intended fatty layer, promoting consistent and reliable subcutaneous delivery of insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e150bb4043de862453b3e96cf6cb663e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74b3171b97fd80965359afadc0af9312</data>
