</node>
<node id="Dose">
  <data key="d0">Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dose is the quantified amount of insulin, expressed in units, that is prescribed by a clinician and subsequently administered to a patient. It represents the number of insulin units selected for injection—whether a whole‑number value, a fractional amount, or a very small dose—and is the fundamental variable that determines both the intensity and duration of the insulin’s action, whether the product is a rapid‑acting formulation such as Humalog or another type such as Novolin® R.

The specific dose is individualized based on a range of patient‑specific factors. Clinicians consider current glucose levels, carbohydrate intake, physical activity, insulin sensitivity, body weight, and the type of diabetes when calculating the appropriate number of units. Because these variables can change over time, the prescribed dose may be adjusted periodically to achieve optimal glycemic control.

In practice, the dose is selected using the dose‑setting mechanism of an insulin delivery device. For example, an insulin pen typically allows the user to choose any value from 1 up to 60 units per injection by turning a dose‑setting button; the chosen amount is then delivered when the pen is activated. The selected number of units—such as the 2 units illustrated in a common example—directly corresponds to the amount of insulin that will enter the patient’s subcutaneous tissue.

Overall, the insulin dose is a tailored prescription that reflects the patient’s therapeutic needs and is administered through the chosen delivery system, with the goal of maintaining blood‑glucose levels within the target range.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8041b0c4fb8dc726149807b6b3e61287&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fec70e5369024cec08c0820152befe39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64d156e12ba8d9418249ea309aab0552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1aba4eb027a027fb1ad6e0105b97cfa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee5400065df79b02b6c04efc0f4854d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e7689ca0fc736d9eae94256f73fed52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0710d91bd443ae1af3fd98049f4ad42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd25c8bd4672601d6ee40e4b7e28a69c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46f4c578332d81d4fc1ca84bcbc26bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9b085ae33d49450f3f8bc342b5eae55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0689fd73d2061ee466b9b90e03bda141&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-929e31613caadc967412041c8c2155b3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/transporte e conservação Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-20201.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319694</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Skin">
  <data key="d0">Skin</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Skin is the body’s outer integument, a protective tissue that covers the entire surface of the body. It must be healthy and is typically cleaned with an alcohol swab before any medical procedure that involves penetration, such as insulin injections. 

When insulin is administered, the needle is inserted through the skin to reach the subcutaneous layer, where the insulin is deposited for subsequent absorption into the bloodstream. The skin therefore serves as the entry point for the needle and the barrier that must be traversed to deliver the medication safely and effectively. 

In addition to insulin delivery, the skin is also the anatomical site on which continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) sensors are placed. The sensors are adhered to the outer skin layer, allowing them to measure interstitial glucose levels while the skin remains intact.&lt;SEP&gt;The outer tissue of the body where an insulin pump is attached for continuous sub‑cutaneous delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the outer layer of the body where the glucose sensor is placed beneath.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd25c8bd4672601d6ee40e4b7e28a69c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb8954aaa1e1230598596ce6f5df249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c24a2af0d0291fc4f4730256c420e93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c6beab51324e055f4a39b388e2eefe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdabdf4730f349f4b46e7242c65d440d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cceea4f7036977c7ddcbe81f76f8aa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-545c19428427ece2c838e352e759e388</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786336918</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Irregular Insulin Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Irregular Insulin Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Irregular insulin absorption, where insulin is taken up too quickly or too slowly, can result from lipo‑hipertrofia.&lt;SEP&gt;Irregular insulin absorption refers to the variable rate at which injected insulin is taken up, which can be excessively fast or slow.&lt;SEP&gt;Irregular Insulin Absorption describes inconsistent uptake of insulin caused by lipo-hipertrofia, leading to glucose control problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f8c8b2b40633cf37993b620002b889</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786320224</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Hypoglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypoglycemia is a medical condition defined by abnormally low blood‑glucose concentrations, most commonly when plasma glucose falls below 70 mg/dL, with severe episodes often classified at levels under 54 mg/dL. The disorder encompasses a spectrum of low‑glucose events—including mild, moderate, severe, nocturnal, post‑prandial, and asymptomatic episodes—that may be detected during routine monitoring or during a four‑hour post‑meal observation period.

The condition is most frequently encountered as an adverse metabolic effect of diabetes treatment, especially when insulin therapy exceeds the body’s glucose needs. Typical precipitants include insulin overdose, inaccurate dose adjustments, pump‑related delivery errors, improper injection technique, missed or delayed meals, unplanned vigorous exercise, alcohol consumption, and certain concomitant drugs that mask symptoms, particularly in older adults. Intensive glycemic‑control strategies and drug‑related adverse effects also increase the risk, and hypoglycemia is recognized as a major limiting factor in such regimens. Inhaled insulin formulations have distinct risk profiles: rapid‑acting Afrezza generally reduces nocturnal low‑glucose events, whereas the earlier Tecnósfera was associated with a modest reduction in the daily duration of low‑glucose episodes. Erroneous claims that insufficient or stopped insulin causes hypoglycemia are not supported by the evidence.

Clinically, hypoglycemia presents along a continuum. Mild episodes produce autonomic warning signs such as sweating, palpitations, anxiety, headache, nausea, confusion, or impaired cognition, which can compromise concentration, reaction time, and safety‑critical tasks like driving. Moderate to severe episodes may progress to marked neuroglycopenia, loss of consciousness, seizures, and, in extreme cases, death. Even modest glucose declines can adversely affect mental performance, especially in older individuals, and many patients experience unrecognized (Level 3) episodes. Situations that heighten risk include alcohol consumption, intense physical activity, and sleep, all of which can lead to hypoglycemia that is difficult for patients to perceive.

Management prioritizes rapid restoration of glucose. For mild cases, immediate ingestion of fast‑acting carbohydrates is recommended; moderate to severe hypoglycemia often requires intravenous dextrose or other emergency glucose‑raising interventions, and corrective insulin dosing may be necessary when excess insulin is the cause. Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems, including flash and retro‑SMCG technologies, have demonstrated efficacy in reducing hypoglycemic events by providing real‑time alarms and data that enable timely corrective actions, particularly in pregnant people with diabetes and in studies of flash glucose monitoring. Insulin pump therapy and modern CGM tools are specifically aimed at preventing or mitigating hypoglycemia. Preventive strategies focus on patient education, careful insulin dosing, awareness of risk factors (exercise timing, nighttime dosing, alcohol use), and the integration of CGM thresholds that keep sensor glucose values above preset limits, thereby protecting cognition, daily functioning, and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypoglycemia is a condition of low blood glucose characterized by symptoms and requiring treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypoglycemia is a medical condition characterized by low blood glucose levels.</data>
