<node id="Hyperglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Hyperglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperglycemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high concentrations of glucose in the bloodstream, typically defined when blood‑glucose values exceed the normal target range (often &gt; 250 mg/dL). It reflects a failure of the body’s glucose‑homeostasis mechanisms and can be identified during routine examinations, serving as a target for early diagnosis and treatment even in individuals without a prior history of diabetes.

The most frequent underlying mechanisms involve inadequate insulin effect, which may arise from B‑cell deficiency or dysfunction, insufficient insulin delivery, missed or mistimed doses of rapid‑acting or basal insulin analogues, dosing errors, or technical problems with insulin‑delivery devices such as pumps. Additional contributors include insulin‑neutralizing antibodies, injection‑site abnormalities (lipodystrophy, lipohypertrophy, cutaneous amyloidosis), increased insulin demand during pregnancy, post‑prandial glucose excursions, rebound hyperglycemia after drug‑induced hypoglycemia, and, in the early post‑transplant period, immunosuppressive‑induced insulin resistance. Rarely, excess insulin delivery can trigger a counter‑regulatory hormone surge.

Clinically, hyperglycemia produces classic osmotic symptoms such as excessive thirst (polydipsia), increased urination (polyuria), and nausea. Cognitive and motor performance may be degraded, raising safety concerns such as unsafe driving. If left untreated, acute complications can develop, most notably ketosis and diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Chronic exposure to high glucose markedly raises the risk of long‑term vascular injury, contributing to renal, neural, and ocular complications and overall increased morbidity in diabetes.

Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) devices frequently generate alarms for hyperglycemia, and clinicians often quantify its burden using Time‑Above‑Range (TAR) metrics. Blood‑glucose trends are reviewed to guide therapeutic decisions, and specific nighttime targets may be set to reduce nocturnal excursions.

Management centers on prompt, individualized monitoring and insulin therapy. Strategies include corrective insulin boluses, adjustment of pump or injection‑site issues, patient education regarding dose timing, pregnancy‑related needs, alarm response, and lifestyle factors. Automated insulin‑delivery systems and clinical protocols are employed to prevent hyperglycemia, and temporary treatment‑plan adjustments are made in collaboration with the diabetes care team when hyperglycemia is detected. By addressing both immediate threats such as DKA and the long‑term sequelae of sustained hyperglycemia, therapeutic approaches aim to maintain glucose within safe limits and reduce morbidity.</data>
