</node>
<node id="Rotation of Injection Sites">
  <data key="d0">Rotation of Injection Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The practice of changing insulin injection locations to prevent complications and ensure consistent absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Rotating injection sites correctly is a practice that helps prevent lipo‑hipertrofia.&lt;SEP&gt;Rotation of injection sites is the systematic alternation of insulin injection locations to prevent tissue changes such as lipohypertrophy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299818</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Mellitus">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by persistently elevated blood‑glucose concentrations (hyperglycemia) that result from inadequate insulin production, impaired insulin utilization, or a combination of both.  The disorder encompasses several clinical forms, the most prevalent being type 1 diabetes, which arises from immune‑mediated destruction of insulin‑producing β‑cells, type 2 diabetes, which involves insulin resistance together with relative insulin deficiency, and gestational diabetes that develops during pregnancy.  Genetic factors also contribute to susceptibility; families carrying AKT2 mutations, for example, exhibit an increased incidence of Diabetes Mellitus, and the condition frequently co‑occurs with metabolic syndrome and lipodystrophies.

Sustained hyperglycemia predisposes individuals to a wide spectrum of acute and long‑term complications.  Cardiovascular disease, diabetic nephropathy, peripheral and autonomic neuropathy, retinopathy, and an elevated risk of infections are among the chronic sequelae.  In patients with longstanding disease, especially those on intensive insulin regimens, the physiological cues that signal hypoglycaemia may become blunted, leading to reduced hypoglycaemia awareness and a heightened danger of severe hypoglycaemic episodes.  Consequently, careful self‑monitoring, patient education, and individualized treatment plans are essential to maintain safe glucose levels.

Management of Diabetes Mellitus relies heavily on insulin therapy to achieve glycaemic control and mitigate the risk of complications.  A broad array of insulin formulations is employed, ranging from rapid‑acting analogues (e.g., NovoRapid, insulin glulisine marketed as APIDRA® and APIDRA® SOLOSTAR®) to premixed preparations (e.g., Humalog Mix 50 KwikPen) and long‑acting basal insulins (e.g., insulin detemir/Levemir® FlexPen®).  Human regular insulins such as Novolin® R and Humulin® R are also available in vials, Penfill® cartridges, and FlexPen® devices.  Insulin pumps and continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems, often complemented by continuous ketone monitoring, provide tighter glycaemic regulation for both type 1 and type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.  These therapeutic strategies are integrated with regular screening and diagnostic protocols that assess glucose levels, HbA1c, and related metabolic parameters to guide treatment adjustments.

Overall, Diabetes Mellitus represents a multifaceted metabolic disorder that demands comprehensive therapeutic approaches—including diverse insulin products, advanced delivery technologies, vigilant monitoring, and personalized care—to address its complex pathophysiology, genetic influences, and the broad range of associated health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus is the chronic metabolic disease that the guideline classifies into several types.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disease discussed throughout the text, encompassing various subtypes and associated genetic syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus is a metabolic disease discussed in the guideline, with various subtypes and associated conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by elevated blood glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A chronic metabolic disease characterized by high blood glucose.</data>
