<node id="Type 1 Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 Diabetes is a chronic autoimmune form of diabetes mellitus in which the immune system selectively destroys pancreatic β‑cells, leading to absolute or severe relative insulin deficiency. The disease is typically identified by the presence of islet autoantibodies—often multiple in children—and may present at any age, although it is most commonly diagnosed in childhood or adolescence; onset before six months of age is rare. Classic clinical features include polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, and a heightened risk of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), while patients also experience vulnerability to hypoglycemia, sometimes with reduced awareness of low‑glucose symptoms.

Because endogenous insulin production is insufficient or absent, individuals with Type 1 Diabetes require lifelong exogenous insulin therapy. Standard care relies on intensive insulin regimens, most often a basal‑bolus approach that pairs long‑acting analogs (e.g., insulin glargine) with rapid‑acting insulin for meals. Advanced delivery methods—including continuous subcutaneous insulin pumps, inhaled insulin formulations, and hybrid closed‑loop systems—are increasingly employed. Effective management also depends on frequent self‑monitoring of blood glucose, carbohydrate counting, and prompt dose adjustments during illness, reduced food intake, or physical activity. Continuous or flash glucose monitoring (CGM) is widely used, especially in pediatric and adolescent populations, and routine ketone testing is recommended to detect early DKA. Injection‑site complications such as lipohypertrophy affect a substantial proportion of patients.

The well‑defined pathophysiology of Type 1 Diabetes makes it the primary cohort for numerous clinical investigations. Landmark trials such as the Diabetes Control and Complications Trial (DCCT) and its follow‑up, the Epidemiology of Diabetes Interventions and Complications (EDIC) study, have evaluated intensive insulin therapy, long‑term microvascular and macrovascular complications, and adjunctive treatments. Large‑scale epidemiologic, technology adoption, and behavioral studies frequently reference Type 1 Diabetes as a target population or comparison group, including research on pregnancy outcomes, cystic fibrosis‑related diabetes, and screening programs. Findings from these studies inform goals such as time‑in‑range, glycemic variability, and the relationship between A1C and glucose levels across diverse adult and pediatric cohorts.

Overall, Type 1 Diabetes is characterized by autoimmune β‑cell destruction, absolute insulin deficiency, and the necessity for continuous insulin replacement combined with diligent glucose monitoring. Optimizing glycemic control through personalized insulin dosing, advanced delivery technologies, and proactive complication prevention remains the central clinical priority for improving long‑term health outcomes in this population.</data>
