<node id="Insulin Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Resistance is a metabolic and physiological condition in which the body’s cells exhibit a reduced responsiveness to insulin, impairing the hormone’s ability to promote glucose uptake and thereby lowering blood glucose levels. This diminished cellular response leads to impaired glucose metabolism, elevated blood glucose concentrations, and is a hallmark and common denominator of type 2 diabetes. The condition can also contribute to cystic fibrosis‑related diabetes (CFRD), particularly when infection and inflammation are present.

Insulin Resistance is closely linked to overweight, obesity, and sedentary lifestyles, making it a major risk factor for the development of type 2 diabetes. Individuals with the condition often require higher insulin doses to achieve adequate glycemic control, influencing clinical decisions such as insulin dosage adjustments, cartridge‑size selection, and therapeutic regimen design. The severity of insulin resistance may be exacerbated by several pathological and pharmacological factors, including protease inhibitors used in antiretroviral therapy, chronic HIV infection, elevated pro‑inflammatory cytokines, lipodystrophy (abnormal fat distribution), and certain genetic or disease states such as Lpf Tipo 5.

Therapeutic strategies aim both to overcome the need for higher insulin doses and to improve cellular insulin sensitivity. Conventional approaches include dose escalation, weight reduction, increased physical activity, and lifestyle modifications. Pharmacologic interventions—such as metreleptin therapy and other agents that reduce inflammation or address underlying conditions—have been reported to enhance insulin responsiveness. Adjusting antiretroviral regimens or treating associated inflammatory states can also mitigate insulin resistance, helping to restore more effective glucose regulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0955cb70a6ce135722726162a9811e78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cd48799a027437b9fe2e1a48b61cb3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6243c304001345b8f15d1659f91051&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86b5a9c60ab13c2016dd454edf634c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003b19adf9ab32e9587902e8d639d701&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383b02cc66391d66174344ff49464766&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c96221c60c19f02ac6efa6a987b1e15b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0932e9c21a330869629b3fa70d2e1f5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06425d1f80d868bd58871b6a6e740102&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e6371d8083fb20b137912cdaeec1b8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4655f823785fa91e72cba96e00dba34c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec7e786a85b1b6e6cc75bcfc9189fd27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4adabacd09697393ca15fc999724c51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c37e26e62468b725533260347fb4e31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cdf6af658e42c2f4f7dca5e4dbdd3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55e58732610596ce9a8f3145d34f75a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab98b1c07f7bbaa3ad28fb83167f489&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba38023866c2db7437e618a60033f21f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a111d4b40ec241cf54126f594a80308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9eb02ab1ab7d834858bf60a8b402e6ea</data>
