<node id="Uncontrolled Glycemia">
  <data key="d0">Uncontrolled Glycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uncontrolled glycemia denotes consistently abnormal blood‑sugar levels, either too high or too low, that increase health risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299819</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retinopathy">
  <data key="d0">Retinopathy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Retinopathy is a microvascular eye complication that affects the retina and is primarily associated with diabetes. It arises from chronic high blood‑sugar levels and is recognized as a diabetes‑related retinal disease that can progress when glycemic control deteriorates, with risk increasing as the proportion of time spent in target glucose range (Time in Range, TIR) decreases. The condition is a common outcome measured in studies of intensive diabetes therapy, particularly in type 1 diabetes, where it is used as an endpoint to assess the benefits of tighter glucose management.

In clinical research and pharmacovigilance, retinopathy is frequently recorded as an ocular adverse event. It has been reported as an adverse reaction in trials involving insulin products such as BASAGLAR and Lantus®, and it appears in safety tables summarizing ocular adverse events in insulin studies. These reports underscore the importance of monitoring retinal health when patients receive insulin therapy.

Preventive strategies for retinopathy include advanced diabetes management techniques. Evidence suggests that insulin pump therapy may help reduce the incidence or progression of retinopathy in youth with diabetes, likely by providing more stable glucose control and improving TIR. Consequently, retinopathy remains a key focus for clinicians aiming to mitigate long‑term visual complications in diabetic populations.&lt;SEP&gt;Retinopathy is an eye problem that can arise as a serious complication of uncontrolled blood sugar.&lt;SEP&gt;Retinopathy is a diabetes‑related eye disease affecting retinal blood vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;Retinopathy is a microvascular complication of diabetes affecting the retina.&lt;SEP&gt;A microvascular complication of diabetes affecting the retina.&lt;SEP&gt;Microvascular damage to the retina commonly occurring in diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;A diabetes complication affecting the eyes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d194d06ef99b3f8aa6b6542b7e93e762&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c580ebfc111aa29796af1a6f15941c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7e6ca9bf63e843b814736c0409c70fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94150b57cc35a773b7c36f33f237f8a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3b81193a603a098b1ff9f6f0f665fec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8393954b021d0ad02018d1a449e0cc93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6df8d05eb3fdd356a1eaef16a26dd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74ef8c063d84e34c44021aa138b9b903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e668c40e3bc21d982ed03a052f0f9339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9eb02ab1ab7d834858bf60a8b402e6ea</data>
