<node id="Cetonas">
  <data key="d0">Cetonas</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetonas are ketone bodies, metabolic compounds generated by the body when glucose availability is insufficient to meet energy needs, such as during prolonged fasting, uncontrolled diabetes, or other states that trigger fat metabolism. In these conditions the liver converts fatty acids into ketone bodies, which then circulate in the blood and can be excreted in the urine.

The presence and concentration of cetonas are commonly measured to evaluate a person’s metabolic status. They can be quantified in urine or blood samples, and the results are used to assess ketosis during intercurrent illness, monitor diabetic complications, and detect the risk of diabetic ketoacidosis. Clinical protocols often call for testing cetonas when capillary glucose exceeds 240 mg/dL or when sensor glucose rises above a high‑threshold value, as elevated glucose together with ketone production signals a heightened danger of ketoacidosis.

A positive ketone test is also interpreted as an indicator that strenuous physical activity should be avoided, because exercise can exacerbate metabolic imbalance in the presence of significant ketonemia. Consequently, regular monitoring of cetonas—through urine dipsticks or blood ketone meters—provides clinicians and patients with actionable information to manage glucose control, prevent acute complications, and guide lifestyle adjustments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46c9571a4df1570d4a097dc949f512cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817893fc39ccf7551d476cefaf7f32ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eaa74c3ef8165f52ddf18e17a240a9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fb90cfe4a3993c8a19c8fd291cc4d67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220ba7466dbb8a2a07b8e4b09b215d39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e0d8e22a06c73855abed11561ce467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dce7a759a9de18a4d1bcc77a071fbe2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786368588</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cetoacidose Diabética">
  <data key="d0">Cetoacidose Diabética</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetoacidose Diabética, also known as diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), is a severe and potentially lethal metabolic complication of diabetes that arises when excessive ketone bodies accumulate in the bloodstream, leading to acidemia. The condition is most commonly associated with untreated or poorly controlled hyperglycemia in type 1 diabetes, although it can also occur in type 2 diabetes under stress or insulin deficiency. Key clinical features include markedly elevated blood glucose levels, high concentrations of ketone bodies—particularly β‑hydroxybutyrate (β‑OHB)—and a drop in blood pH, reflecting the underlying ketoacidosis.

The pathophysiology of Cetoacidose Diabética involves prolonged hyperglycemia that drives lipolysis and hepatic ketogenesis, resulting in the buildup of acetoacetate, acetone, and β‑OHB. This accumulation overwhelms the body's buffering capacity, producing metabolic acidosis that can rapidly become life‑threatening if not promptly treated. Typical manifestations include polyuria, polydipsia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid breathing (Kussmaul respirations), and altered mental status. Laboratory findings confirm hyperglycemia, ketonemia/ketonuria, and a low arterial pH.

Management requires urgent correction of dehydration, electrolyte imbalances, and hyperglycemia, usually with intravenous fluids, insulin infusion, and careful monitoring of potassium levels. It is important to note that certain insulin formulations, such as BASAGLAR KWIKPEN, are not considered the preferred therapy for treating Cetoacidose Diabética, emphasizing the need for appropriate insulin regimens in acute care settings. Prompt recognition and treatment are essential to reduce morbidity and mortality associated with this grave diabetic complication.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetoacidose diabética is a severe complication caused by high levels of ketones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2db4ee43ed7cc39546184320e1328d06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a247846b9ec5d44d0bcbe6c5f15599f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45ee3dcb366c1301c9fb1d2ce9f323c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb0cd81f0439cbb74e3c0ff54236c45d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbe87fd92ad66dcb02b25b669d829f6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfa23fc15cc4d520a32488843dbd710b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276b766b8d515b1e7f388b5b7381bfbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e0d8e22a06c73855abed11561ce467</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/cgm.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx</data>
