<node id="Blood Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Blood Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood Glucose is the physiological measurement of the concentration of glucose—the primary circulating sugar—in the bloodstream, most commonly expressed in milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL). It represents the amount of glucose present in blood and is regulated continuously by the hormones insulin and glucagon to keep levels within a relatively narrow target range. In many languages the term appears as “glicemia” or “glicose,” but it always denotes the same metric.

Because tight glucose control is essential for people with diabetes, Blood Glucose is routinely measured to guide therapeutic decisions. Typical measurement methods include finger‑stick capillary tests performed with a blood‑glucose (BG) meter, which provide rapid point‑of‑care values, as well as laboratory assays and continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems. BG meter readings are used to calibrate CGM devices, serve as reference values when sensor‑derived data are unavailable, and inform insulin dosing. They also help detect hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) or hyperglycemia (high blood sugar) and allow clinicians and patients to evaluate overall diabetes management and long‑term metabolic control.

In the context of insulin‑pump therapy, Blood Glucose values are integral to the pump’s monitoring algorithms. The pump may request a BG reading in response to alerts, alarms, or specific operating modes such as SmartGuard and Manual mode, especially when sensor‑derived glucose (SG) values are missing. Users are instructed to check their Blood Glucose promptly after such alerts to ensure safe insulin delivery and to adjust pump settings as needed.

Overall, Blood Glucose serves as a fundamental clinical parameter for both home self‑management and professional care. It provides the quantitative basis for adjusting insulin therapy, preventing acute glucose excursions, and supporting long‑term health outcomes in individuals whose glucose regulation is impaired.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Glucose is the concentration of glucose in the blood, elevated in Type 2 Diabetes and referenced in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1700b8f7c792afbd394001e28bf632f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7715667f5fca178f0988984e932ecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5913d17c337be62cd526d46fbdcd9a6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2f8a0d67f7320d6157b61dbbf428a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3d901ee668c4fdbd518454aa3ebea3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff2787d1cec4a771ee14b7d5d97324a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bb94d08bdb958eff25f76c9a6ac18a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b53b69acbef0e172ea7719cc15bf3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-177e9aca0bf1c1724474ea529826bece&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92417ae942a11c591feb2ee9b5fadd7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a01dc8dc15b92e2b0b570753b0f57632&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71de01baf40871c616eaa39cb8189f43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c5a5c97986d9b31b155b0c8f0cac1c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f94cd06ab0d1bcc7a9349bc40ea3eb22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0932e9c21a330869629b3fa70d2e1f5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06425d1f80d868bd58871b6a6e740102&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-545c19428427ece2c838e352e759e388&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-664916c2444f157f5d7d9bc41b032d26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0645201ac55eff389c44997aecbb6ce7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c37e26e62468b725533260347fb4e31</data>
