</node>
<node id="Physician">
  <data key="d0">Physician</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A medical professional who downloads and analyzes data from blind continuous glucose sensors.&lt;SEP&gt;Physician is a medical doctor referenced as the professional to consult for dosage adjustments and medication guidance.&lt;SEP&gt;Physician is a medical professional who should be consulted when other drugs are used with Humalog Mix 25 Kwikpen.&lt;SEP&gt;Physician is the medical professional who should be consulted before combining other drugs with Humalog Mix 50 Kwikpen.&lt;SEP&gt;A physician is a medical professional who should be consulted by patients before traveling or adjusting insulin therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59c9bf7f429a82cbfd7083e36ff8f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ce520f0fe7dc4a6c79f92a25dfaff19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd94288932ad6675a1de2b46b3fd9128&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318528</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patient">
  <data key="d0">Patient</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Patient is any individual who is receiving insulin therapy for diabetes mellitus, encompassing adults, adolescents and children (generally over one year of age) who have been instructed in the correct technique for administering insulin. The definition includes persons who self‑inject insulin at home using a variety of delivery devices such as FlexPen®, KwikPen®, SoloStar® pen or other pre‑filled pens, and who are taught proper needle disposal, site rotation and injection‑site hygiene. It also covers individuals who use more advanced delivery systems, including the MiniMed™ insulin pump, the MiniMed™ 640G system with its sensor and transmitter, and glucose‑monitoring devices such as the Accu‑Chek Solo. In addition, patients may be prescribed specific insulin products including rapid‑acting analogues (e.g., Humalog (insulin lispro), NovoRapid®, BASAGLAR), intermediate‑acting or human insulins such as Novolin® R and Humulin R, as well as inhaled insulin (Afrezza) when appropriate.

Patients are required to engage in continuous self‑management. This involves regular blood‑glucose monitoring with glucometers or continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems, recognition of hypoglycemia symptoms, carrying fast‑acting carbohydrates, and making informed adjustments to insulin dose in response to lifestyle changes, disease progression, medication interactions or programmed pump settings. Dose titration may be performed under the guidance of healthcare professionals, and patients are advised to consult clinicians for individualized dose modifications and to address adverse reaction risks.

Ongoing education is a core component of patient care. Training reinforces proper injection technique, safe use of pens, pumps and sensors, correct disposal of sharps, and strategies for dose adjustment. Guidance also emphasizes adherence to clinical recommendations, regular device checks, and the importance of maintaining glycemic control to reduce complications. The patient role therefore integrates medication administration, device operation, self‑monitoring, and collaborative communication with healthcare providers to achieve optimal diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient is the individual addressed as “você”, who may have type 1 diabetes, experience frequent hypoglycemia, and use insulin multiple times daily.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient is an individual with diabetes who uses SMCG devices for glucose monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient is the individual undergoing glucose testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d0de4d525c273cda4939a501eaecc6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74b3171b97fd80965359afadc0af9312&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5b4c60b3de8c56d9ab869d868345fb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb10c00517fa772be2ca9a42f497edf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9725072a361e9b1b97c5daa35e061e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b9c41c968bcc1d46185583661e8f989&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ce520f0fe7dc4a6c79f92a25dfaff19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffe095bda1b787c5b5ae2d61a2f74c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb8954aaa1e1230598596ce6f5df249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df616353e032c899b02bfa4368bc2667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fa819f7e1e42eb688d4894dc3ca7e64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ac99e7359716e2cecc2ec93159218b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a1bb820381073ad2475541a3a575&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2f8a0d67f7320d6157b61dbbf428a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75ab69ff55dee7eb424c572ea28a45ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eedfa9db50d515c53181256a5959a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4655f823785fa91e72cba96e00dba34c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63f18f20af642d4a480c16e7a6780bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ac99b8588a8c480f44a5519509f7713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6601fea85bfbb9f20040c70b7e21695d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/IFU Accu-Chek Guide Solo Diabetes Manager.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/glicemias capilares.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377323</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nurse">
  <data key="d0">Nurse</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A healthcare provider who assists the patient in diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Nurse is a health professional trained by Elliott P. Joslin to educate diabetes patients on insulin use.&lt;SEP&gt;Nurse is the healthcare professional who also instructs the insertion of the needle and dose selection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd25c8bd4672601d6ee40e4b7e28a69c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786316115</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nocturnal Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Nocturnal Hypoglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nocturnal hypoglycemia is a condition characterized by episodes of low blood glucose that occur while a person is asleep. These nighttime hypoglycemic events are typically identified through continuous glucose monitoring or by measuring glucose concentrations that fall below 36 mg/dL during the sleep period, often between the administration of bedtime insulin and the subsequent fasting glucose assessment.  

In clinical studies, nocturnal hypoglycemia is reported as part of the overall symptomatic hypoglycemia event rate and is considered a key safety outcome when evaluating insulin therapies. For example, comparative trials have shown that the incidence of nocturnal hypoglycemia is lower with insulin glargine (Lantus®) than with NPH insulin, highlighting its relevance in treatment decisions.  

Overall, nocturnal hypoglycemia refers to any low‑glucose episode occurring during the night, regardless of detection method, and is an important metric for assessing the risk profile of diabetes management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Nocturnal Hypoglycemia is low blood glucose occurring during sleep, more common with some insulin types.&lt;SEP&gt;Nocturnal hypoglycemia refers to low blood sugar events occurring during sleep, a safety concern in insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Nocturnal Hypoglycemia refers to low blood‑glucose episodes occurring during sleep, an outcome measured in the ASPIRE trial.&lt;SEP&gt;Nocturnal Hypoglycemia is the occurrence of low blood glucose during sleep.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afc9722167670b0bfeef0b43f4b0df79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e9c7394777a2e452b57285541f75f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d648fb4d9a20040dd617a7ca164c9f10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a592aedb257bad9532268b4056ec15c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf25fca295be60e28b7c7d3103f722fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d9c944d51a42525c017d9109f46684c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14772c7de5db762c492a75a1c744dae6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68b98187c6ab15468f1c0d8f5e7f9f6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f272d1c9c38e2a6b6d02b5aa66809e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cd925790899146eeb8a373879b5feac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e99a265d5e53a3da6b755599d395791</data>
