<node id="ADA">
  <data key="d0">ADA</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The American Diabetes Association (ADA) is a health‑focused organization that develops and disseminates clinical guidelines and recommendations for the management of diabetes. It is commonly referenced by its abbreviation “ADA” in medical literature and manuals.  

Among its many activities, the ADA issues comprehensive diabetes guidelines that cover testing, treatment, and target metrics. Central to these guidelines is the use of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) as a key measure of long‑term glucose control, with a recommended target of ≤7.5% for many patients. The association also specifies fasting and post‑meal glucose limits to help clinicians set appropriate glycemic goals.  

The ADA’s guidance extends to special populations, including pregnant women with diabetes, for whom it provides specific glycemic target recommendations to promote maternal and fetal health. Additionally, the organization offers support resources for insulin‑pump users, including a contact phone number for assistance.  

Beyond guideline development, the ADA authors and co‑authors numerous peer‑reviewed publications that contribute to the evidence base for diabetes care. Its role as a leading authority makes the ADA a central reference point for clinicians, researchers, and patients seeking reliable information on diabetes management.</data>
