  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brazil">
  <data key="d0">Brazil</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Brazil is a country in South America that serves as the setting for a range of health‑related observations and developments. In the Brazilian public health system, known as the Sistema Único de Saúde (SUS), patients report difficulty obtaining blood‑ketone test strips, indicating gaps in the availability of diabetes monitoring supplies. At the same time, Brazil is noted as the nation where the use of syringes for insulin administration remains the most common delivery method, reflecting prevailing insulin‑administration practices across the country.

The nation is also highlighted in the context of infectious disease and pharmaceutical regulation. Brazil was the location where the Zika virus first arrived in the region, and medication information related to that outbreak is provided within the country. Brazil houses the national regulatory agency Agência Nacional de Vigilância Sanitária (ANVISA) as well as the pharmaceutical and medical‑device companies that interact with it. Through ANVISA, the fast‑acting insulin analog Fiasp has obtained regulatory approval for use in Brazil.

Additional medical‑device introductions are taking place in Brazil. The Accu‑Chek® Solo glucose‑monitoring system is slated for introduction, and the associated Accu‑Chek Solo insulin‑pump is expected to become available in the near future, expanding therapeutic options for Brazilian patients with diabetes. All of these points collectively portray Brazil as a central venue for both challenges and advancements in diabetes care, infectious‑disease response, and regulatory activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where FreeStyle Libre is commonly used.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is the country where FreeStyle Libre, the insulin infusion pump, and other continuous glucose monitoring technologies are marketed.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil offers point‑of‑care HbA1c testing as a feasible option for glycemic assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where universal screening for pre‑clinical DM1 is not currently recommended.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is the country where the Ministry of Health, Vigitel report, and related organizations are based.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d795dc393eb02c6418957ee1678504f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4f2935f492529f0d551140b53a84fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fef140d95c1acc260149fd1d20f7853&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7387e81b8141435df1372f5d8b27662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fe5eab149567b7d1c155f61afdb5a34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0a2dce3852e38344f91c86f45dda403&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08c7f7bb0209cabd75e27f9c8556c214&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93594146e1ebe099cfc47906e940939f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f716359c14483f9be36a73861d75008&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cf24d486949a568d61c1de206cd3e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a05276c5db43e3a1e9f987056012750f</data>
