</node>
<node id="Infection">
  <data key="d0">Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infection (medical condition) is a health disorder that arises when pathogenic microorganisms invade and multiply within the body’s tissues. By definition, it encompasses bacterial, viral, fungal, or parasitic agents that can disrupt normal physiological processes. The presence of infection is recognized as an indication for blood‑ketone testing because acute illness often alters metabolic pathways, and infection can serve as a stressor that precipitates diabetic ketoacidosis, particularly in individuals with type 2 diabetes. It also contributes to markedly elevated blood‑glucose levels and can impair insulin sensitivity, thereby affecting overall glycemic control during acute illness. Infection is listed among the conditions that increase the likelihood of hospitalization for people with diabetes, and it has been identified as a factor that can exacerbate insulin resistance in patients with cystic fibrosis. Post‑transplant patients may experience infection as a medical complication, and the condition can further compromise graft outcomes.

From a clinical‑practice perspective, infection of the skin is a contraindication for injection procedures because contaminated skin can introduce pathogens into deeper tissues. The risk of infection is heightened by the reuse or sharing of needles and insulin pens, and contaminated injection equipment poses a direct health hazard. In the context of diabetes therapy, infection has been reported as an adverse event associated with the rapid‑acting insulin Humalog, occurring in at least 5 % of patients with type 1 or type 2 diabetes receiving the medication. Beyond human health, infection has also been noted as a source of bacterial contamination that hampered experiments involving laboratory dogs and as a disease condition that disrupted work during hot weather despite careful preventive measures.

Infection (journal) is an academic periodical dedicated to publishing research on infectious diseases. The journal features studies such as J. Beyer’s article “Amphotericin B aerosols for preventing pulmonary aspergillosis,” contributing to the scientific literature on prevention and treatment of fungal infections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e150bb4043de862453b3e96cf6cb663e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d26ce64e79b8c4a147140be2f9c63dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2943becc796c8ce3f0aaedf0978c3213&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee5400065df79b02b6c04efc0f4854d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffe095bda1b787c5b5ae2d61a2f74c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-649c4ac605d59d792f3d5f8952251312&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eff48c1408719e81f1f423f76dfd19e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-172719b5694ed1fb8003b6874395d641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fe769b03fc66d4a691f3b7f741b92cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345ad64f464691cc28341ccd246032db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2cd796639bdb0e10eeda7ef3d9ebbce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e7eef761aec994cc4558b2db5e1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cdf6af658e42c2f4f7dca5e4dbdd3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a111d4b40ec241cf54126f594a80308</data>
