<node id="Cotton">
  <data key="d0">Cotton</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cotton is a material commonly employed in the preparation and administration of insulin injections. In the post‑injection phase, a piece of cotton—often in the form of a cotton pad—is pressed against the injection site for about five seconds after the needle is withdrawn, providing gentle compression to help minimize bleeding and reassure the patient.

In addition to compression, cotton is used to manage residual fluid at the injection site. The material readily absorbs excess solution and can be dabbed on the skin to dry the area after it has been disinfected, ensuring the site is clean and free of moisture before the injection is given. Cotton is typically gathered during the preparation steps and may be used to dab the site after the initial disinfection with an alcohol swab.

Cotton also plays a role in the aseptic handling of insulin vials. When soaked in 70 % isopropyl or ethyl alcohol, cotton is used to wipe and disinfect the rubber stopper or cap of the vial, as well as to clean the injection site itself. This combined cotton‑and‑alcohol method helps reduce the risk of microbial contamination before drawing up the insulin solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8768fa32f88d682aa4ddf677297047e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6a26a5d2c41087b58a0f7f71ad21cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8310c065da1c624d58d9a0a8e9ff7d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301826</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Rigid Deposit">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Rigid Deposit</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A plastic rigid deposit (e.g., a bottle for fabric softener or disinfectant) is used to collect used needles and lancets for safe disposal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299831</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Health Post">
  <data key="d0">Health Post</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A health post is the nearest health facility where the plastic deposit containing used sharps is delivered.&lt;SEP&gt;Health posts are community health facilities that provide collection containers for safe needle disposal.&lt;SEP&gt;Health posts are locations where collection bins for used syringes are available.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Post is a community health facility that may host collection points for needle disposal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9725072a361e9b1b97c5daa35e061e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df616353e032c899b02bfa4368bc2667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cd48799a027437b9fe2e1a48b61cb3d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318529</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hand Washing">
  <data key="d0">Hand Washing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hand washing with water and soap is the first step in the insulin injection procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;The step of washing hands with water and soap before preparing the insulin injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8768fa32f88d682aa4ddf677297047e</data>
