<node id="Edema">
  <data key="d0">Edema</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Edema is the pathological accumulation of fluid in the skin and underlying tissues that manifests as swelling. In the setting of diabetes treatment, edema is recognized as an adverse reaction to insulin therapy and to certain oral antidiabetic agents, most notably the thiazolidinediones (TZDs), which promote sodium and fluid retention. The swelling may be localized to an insulin injection site or may appear more diffusely as peripheral edema of the lower extremities. Injection‑site edema has been reported, though relatively uncommonly, with a range of insulin products, including rapid‑acting analogues such as insulin aspart (Novorapid), basal formulations such as insulin glargine (Lantus®) and its biosimilar BASAGLAR, long‑acting insulins such as insulin degludec (Tresiba®) and insulin detemir (Levemir®), as well as human regular insulin (Novolin® R and Novolin® R Penfill®). The reaction is often transient, may occur more frequently at the start of therapy, and is noted especially in patients receiving intensified insulin regimens, those with poor prior metabolic control, or individuals with concurrent congestive heart failure.

The presence of edema can interfere with proper insulin absorption, prompting clinicians to inspect prospective injection sites, avoid areas with fluid accumulation, and adjust injection technique or site rotation. In some cases the swelling may contraindicate use of a particular site, necessitate changes in the overall therapeutic regimen, or lead to dosing revisions to reduce discomfort while preserving glycaemic control. Fluid retention is a broader peripheral effect of insulin‑related sodium retention and may result in generalized lower‑extremity edema beyond the injection locale.

Edema is therefore a clinically noteworthy signal that medication choices, dosing strategies, and injection practices should be reassessed when it occurs, particularly in patients on combined TZD‑insulin therapy. Monitoring for this adverse effect is recommended to minimize fluid retention, improve patient comfort, and ensure effective diabetes management. (Portuguese: “Acúmulo de líquidos nos tecidos, risco associado ao uso de TZD com insulina.”)</data>
