<node id="Adolescents">
  <data key="d0">Adolescents</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adolescents, in the context of diabetes care, refer to the patient group encompassing individuals typically aged 13 to 17 years and, in some clinical definitions, up to 18 years old. This age range captures patients who are in puberty and therefore experience physiological changes that affect insulin requirements. Compared with younger children, adolescents generally need higher insulin doses per kilogram of body weight, reflecting increased insulin resistance that accompanies hormonal development.

A distinctive characteristic of many adolescents with diabetes is low subcutaneous fat. Because of this, clinicians often advise the use of a subcutaneous fold technique when administering insulin injections, ensuring that the needle remains within the subcutaneous tissue and reduces the risk of intramuscular delivery. This consideration is especially relevant for short‑acting formulations such as insulin degludec and rapid‑acting analogues.

Clinical experience with certain newer insulin products is limited in this population. For example, adolescents up to 18 years old have been identified as a group lacking extensive clinical data for insulin degludec, and dosage monitoring is specifically recommended when they are treated with Tresiba (insulin degludec). Conversely, adolescents have been included in pharmacokinetic studies of other insulins, such as Novolin® R, providing valuable data on absorption and action profiles for this age bracket.

Beyond pharmacologic issues, adolescents present unique psychosocial and practical challenges in diabetes management. Family support, or the lack thereof, and broader psychosocial factors can complicate the effective use of insulin pumps. These factors may influence adherence, pump programming, and overall glycemic control, making comprehensive, multidisciplinary support essential for this patient group.&lt;SEP&gt;A population group in which hypoglycemia episodes are often related to alcohol consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Individuals in the teenage years, a population group with specific risk and screening criteria for type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Adolescents are a youth group highlighted for increasing type 2 diabetes rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Adolescents refer to individuals in the teenage age range, a population in which CFRD occurs in about 20%.</data>
