<node id="Pregnant Women">
  <data key="d0">Pregnant Women</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Pregnant women are adult patients who are carrying a fetus and may require insulin therapy during pregnancy. Clinical guidance identifies this group as a specific patient population for whom special insulin administration recommendations apply, particularly in the first and second trimesters.  

For safety, pregnant women are advised to use a subcutaneous fold when injecting insulin, a technique that helps ensure proper subcutaneous delivery and reduces the risk of complications. This recommendation is emphasized in insulin administration guidelines that address the unique physiological changes of pregnancy.  

When prescribing rapid‑acting insulin analogues such as Humalog, the guidance stresses that pregnant women should receive the medication only under direct medical supervision because clinical data on its safety in pregnancy are limited. Similarly, the use of APIDRA (insulin glulisine) is cautioned against without explicit medical guidance, reflecting the overall limited safety data for many insulin products in this population.  

Overall, pregnant women constitute a distinct patient group in insulin therapy protocols, requiring tailored injection techniques and close medical oversight to protect both maternal and fetal health.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnant Women are the specific patient population studied in Study NN 304-1687 for type 1 diabetes treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Women who are pregnant and need professional evaluation before starting MiniMed 780G therapy.</data>
