</node>
<node id="Adult">
  <data key="d0">Adult</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An adult individual receiving insulin injections, for whom skin thickness rarely exceeds 3 mm and short needles are effective.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult is an individual for whom a 45°‑90° injection angle is recommended when using 6‑8 mm needles.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult refers to an adult patient for whom the insulin injection angle of 90 degrees is recommended.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult refers to patients aged 18 and older who may be prescribed Lantus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8768fa32f88d682aa4ddf677297047e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-572e715aada476f4dac69a032e16cc09</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786304212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Child">
  <data key="d0">Child</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A child (up to 6 years old) with thin skin who must always use short needles of 4, 5, or 6 mm together with a subcutaneous fold.&lt;SEP&gt;Child is an individual for whom a 45° injection angle is recommended regardless of needle length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786300283</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adolescent">
  <data key="d0">Adolescent</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An adolescent recipient of insulin who, like children, should use short needles and may benefit from a subcutaneous fold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299840</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pregnant Person">
  <data key="d0">Pregnant Person</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A pregnant individual who may inject insulin in the abdomen during the first two trimesters using a 4 mm needle, subcutaneous fold, and a 45° angle, but should avoid abdominal injections in the third trimester.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1786299840</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baby">
  <data key="d0">Baby</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The fetus or newborn potentially affected by insulin injection practices during pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The baby is the offspring of a pregnant patient with diabetes, whose health depends on proper glucose control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3daabbd42055dc30154f5f97c85fb7d</data>
