<node id="Insulin Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Therapy is a treatment regimen that involves the regular administration of insulin to manage diabetes. It encompasses the full course of care, from the initial initiation of insulin to subsequent intensification phases, and is employed for both type 1 diabetes mellitus (DM1) and type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM2).  

The therapy requires patients to use proper injection techniques or, alternatively, to deliver insulin via an insulin pump, and it typically includes systematic blood‑glucose monitoring so that dosages can be adjusted in response to glucose readings. Monitoring is essential for achieving adequate glycemic control and for tailoring the regimen to individual needs.  

Insulin Therapy is often introduced within the first five years after a diabetes diagnosis, especially when oral agents are insufficient. In type 2 diabetes, higher insulin doses may be required to overcome insulin resistance, while in type 1 diabetes the therapy is essential from the outset.  

Overall, Insulin Therapy represents a comprehensive, patient‑centered approach that combines medication delivery, dose titration, and ongoing glucose surveillance to maintain target blood‑sugar levels and reduce the risk of diabetes‑related complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin Therapy is the preferred treatment for hyperglycemia, PTDM, and preexisting diabetes in the hospital setting.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin therapy involves administering insulin to manage blood glucose levels.</data>
