</node>
<node id="Intermediate‑Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Intermediate‑Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of insulin with moderate onset and duration, commonly mixed with rapid‑acting insulin for immediate glucose control.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate‑acting insulin is a class of insulin with moderate duration, referenced when discussing transfer to Levemir®.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate‑Acting Insulin is a slower‑acting insulin used for basal coverage when delivered by injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin (e.g., NPH) that begins in 1–3 hours, peaks 4–10 hours, and lasts 10–18 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate‑acting insulin is an insulin category with onset 2–4 hours after injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ace26fdb0244e3d0f714a007c9bbb51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c6beab51324e055f4a39b388e2eefe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0932e9c21a330869629b3fa70d2e1f5e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786332449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rapid‑Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Rapid‑Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rapid‑Acting Insulin is a class of fast‑acting insulin analogues and formulations that provide an immediate, short‑duration glucose‑lowering effect. These products are typically administered shortly before meals to cover the rapid rise in blood glucose that follows food intake, and they are also used for correction (bolus) doses when glucose levels exceed target ranges. In clinical practice, rapid‑acting insulin is often combined with intermediate‑acting insulin—such as in mixed regimens—or prescribed alongside basal agents like Levemir® to achieve both basal and prandial control.

The defining pharmacologic profile of rapid‑acting insulin includes a quick onset of action (usually within 10–15 minutes), a peak effect around one hour, and a total duration of action of three to five hours. This profile applies to several marketed products, including the fast‑onset formulation of Novolin® R, as well as newer insulin analogues that are specifically labeled for meal boluses. Because of its rapid pharmacokinetics, rapid‑acting insulin is the preferred choice for delivery via insulin pumps, where precise, minute‑by‑minute glucose regulation is required.

In therapy planning, rapid‑acting insulin is considered essential for achieving tight post‑prandial control, yet its effect is not directly represented by the “Active Insulin Time” setting used in many pump algorithms; clinicians must therefore account for its distinct kinetic properties when configuring pump parameters. Overall, rapid‑acting insulin serves as a critical component of intensive insulin regimens, providing the flexibility needed for both scheduled meal coverage and on‑demand corrective dosing.&lt;SEP&gt;Short‑duration insulin (e.g., Lispro, Aspart) that begins acting within 10–20 minutes and peaks in 1–2 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Rapid‑acting insulin is an insulin category that begins to work within about 15 minutes after injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Rapid‑acting insulin is a fast‑onset insulin formulation used for immediate glucose control after meals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77dc1a2378f763ffabe8fa379881d1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ace26fdb0244e3d0f714a007c9bbb51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1124e7acf6619a1a7c60febd5211c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c96221c60c19f02ac6efa6a987b1e15b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c6beab51324e055f4a39b388e2eefe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff2787d1cec4a771ee14b7d5d97324a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae3b43add031516d14e51c6d2a985bd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0932e9c21a330869629b3fa70d2e1f5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e6371d8083fb20b137912cdaeec1b8b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/LIPODISTROFIAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786333244</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rapid‑Acting Insulin Analogue">
  <data key="d0">Rapid‑Acting Insulin Analogue</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A modified rapid‑acting insulin analogue that can be mixed with intermediate‑acting insulin while preserving stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301844</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combination Insulin Injection Method">
  <data key="d0">Combination Insulin Injection Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The clinical practice of mixing two insulin types in the same syringe to reduce injection frequency, requiring skill, appropriate equipment, and careful technique.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301844</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle Dwell Time">
  <data key="d0">Needle Dwell Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The recommended period the needle remains in subcutaneous tissue after insulin delivery (minimum 5 seconds for a syringe, 10 seconds for a pen) to prevent reflux.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle Dwell Time is the required period (minimum 6 seconds) that a needle remains under the skin to ensure full dose delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0955cb70a6ce135722726162a9811e78</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318500</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alcohol 70% Solution">
  <data key="d0">Alcohol 70% Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A disinfectant solution used to aseptically clean the rubber stopper of an insulin vial before needle insertion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301844</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rubber Stopper">
  <data key="d0">Rubber Stopper</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The rubber seal on an insulin vial that is cleaned with alcohol prior to needle penetration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64b999805e924800faef3b856e45d8bf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786301845</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battery">
  <data key="d0">Battery</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ed0b3e64751c1a28d72e087f711cc20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eedfa9db50d515c53181256a5959a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a9714e3b2aaad60242e3827215230bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-474703bb16e6e26e009e9d433c3d3647&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db8e2f5011e0e0fe7dc11273e2f345b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28515a0aca492eeee478cd9947226498&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d819d6498d4482286628946e37a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec9cae555a06ac660e6c16d90116c8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a111edb31d6739acf013ce812b952366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd8af150d14c8fd9fd3b20e486220f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4d222786e6ed163a90874659266c72a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3895d65c9a22c257a4717f300d4086b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a0307c83d7b514ca00334e4c8ca2357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd74224daea51112742135154e46244&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f94cd06ab0d1bcc7a9349bc40ea3eb22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4faffc449b4b8ea09acbae135d21baa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fba8ea0c6bdb8427804ba05f9fe49f9</data>
  <data key="d2">Battery is the removable power source that supplies electrical energy to a range of diabetes management devices, most notably insulin pumps such as the MiniMed series (including the MiniMed 780G) and the Accu‑Chek Solo, as well as the associated transmitter that communicates pump data. In these devices the battery is typically an AA cell, though the specific chemistry may vary; it must be inserted through a battery cap and can be replaced when its charge becomes low.

The battery’s primary function is to drive the pump’s motor, control electronics, and any user‑interface features such as display brightness and backlighting. Because visual settings consume power, higher brightness can shorten the battery’s usable life. When the charge falls below an acceptable threshold, the device issues low‑battery alerts, and for the MiniMed 780G the battery status must be shown as green before a software update can be initiated. In the transmitter, the battery is rechargeable and must be re‑charged at least once within every 24‑hour period to maintain continuous communication.

Battery disposal follows standard medical‑device guidelines: spent batteries from devices like the Accu‑Chek Solo should be taken to an appropriate collection point for safe disposal. Failure of the battery, whether due to depletion or malfunction, can trigger device alerts and may temporarily halt insulin delivery or data transmission until the battery is replaced or recharged.&lt;SEP&gt;Battery supplies power to the insulin pump and its infusion set.</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/IFU Accu-Chek Guide Solo Diabetes Manager.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786330169</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
