<node id="Glucagon">
  <data key="d0">Glucagon</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucagon is a peptide hormone that functions as a physiological antagonist to insulin, counteracting insulin’s glucose‑lowering effect and helping to prevent or correct hypoglycemia. When insulin levels fall (insulinopenia), glucagon secretion rises, signaling the liver to release stored glucose through glycogenolysis and to generate new glucose via gluconeogenesis, thereby raising blood glucose concentrations and maintaining normal glucose levels during periods when insulin activity might otherwise cause an excessive drop.

In clinical practice, glucagon is formulated as an injectable medication used as a rescue treatment for severe hypoglycemia, especially when oral carbohydrate intake is not possible or the patient cannot swallow. It is typically supplied in doses of 0.5 – 1 mg and can be administered intramuscularly, subcutaneously, or intravenously by trained personnel. This rapid‑acting delivery restores normal glucose levels and is critical in emergencies that may present with seizures, loss of consciousness, coma, or other neurologic complications. Glucagon is commonly included in emergency kits for individuals at risk of level 2 or level 3 hypoglycemia and is prescribed for those who may experience recurrent hypoglycemia, allowing short‑term relaxation of glycemic targets.

Glucagon is also incorporated into dual‑hormone pump therapies, where it is paired with insulin to provide a more physiologic approach to glucose regulation. Additionally, glucagon has been observed to reduce the hypoglycemic effect of certain long‑acting insulin analogs, such as insulin glargine (Apidra Solostar), further mitigating insulin‑induced hypoglycemia when needed.</data>
