<node id="Diabetes Mellitus Type 2">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a chronic, progressive metabolic disorder that primarily affects adults but can also be observed in adolescents. It is characterized by elevated blood glucose levels resulting from insulin resistance and a relative deficiency of insulin secretion. The disease manifests as a persistent hyperglycaemic state that predisposes individuals to long‑term microvascular complications such as retinopathy, nephropathy, and neuropathy, as well as macrovascular complications including coronary artery disease, stroke, and peripheral arterial disease.

Because of its progressive nature, the management of Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 follows a stepwise, patient‑centred approach. Initial therapy typically involves lifestyle modification and oral antidiabetic agents; when glycaemic targets are not met, treatment advances to injectable options. Short‑acting insulin analogues (e.g., Humalog) address acute glucose excursions, while basal insulins—insulin glargine (Lantus®), insulin degludec (Tresiba®/Tresira), insulin glargine‑U300 (Toujeo), and insulin detemir (Levemir®)—provide steady background coverage. These agents may be used alone or in combination with oral drugs, other non‑insulin injectables, or glucagon‑like peptide‑1 (GLP‑1) receptor agonists to optimise control and minimise adverse events.

Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 is a frequent focus of clinical research. Trials have enrolled adult cohorts ranging from dozens to several hundred participants—for example, 63 patients in an insulin‑mixture study and 456 patients in Fiasp® investigations—evaluating the efficacy and safety of basal and rapid‑acting insulins, delivery systems (such as BASAGLAR pens), and combination regimens. The disease’s relevance across diverse study populations underscores its central role in therapeutic development for both insulin‑dependent and non‑insulin‑dependent treatment strategies.

Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems are increasingly employed by patients with Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 to improve glycaemic awareness, yet their broader adoption is limited by sensor cost and insertion discomfort. Despite these barriers, CGM remains an important adjunct in the comprehensive management of this pervasive metabolic disorder, which continues to be refined through extensive research aimed at improving long‑term outcomes for the adult and adolescent populations.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes, a chronic condition for which AMGC may be used with less intensive measurement frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes in older adults, discussed in relation to HbA1c thresholds and mortality risk.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2 is a chronic disease whose early detection can reduce the risk of complications.&lt;SEP&gt;A chronic metabolic disease characterized by high blood glucose, prevalent among children and adolescents, often asymptomatic, requiring screening.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a form of diabetes often associated with obesity, insulin resistance, and later onset.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 is a form of diabetes that may present with normal or elevated Peptídeo C levels, associated with insulin resistance.</data>
