<node id="Surgical Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Surgical Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Surgical procedure is listed among adverse events with Humalog.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical procedure is an adverse event reported in ≥5% of patients with type 1 and type 2 diabetes using Humalog.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d26ce64e79b8c4a147140be2f9c63dd</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786303799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Common Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common reactions occur in 1% to 10% of patients using Humalog and include several specific effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786303799</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allergic Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Allergic Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Allergic reaction is a common reaction to Humalog, potentially occurring at the injection site or systemically.&lt;SEP&gt;Allergic reaction is a common reaction (1‑10% of patients) to Humalog.&lt;SEP&gt;Allergic reaction denotes immediate‑type hypersensitivity to insulin or its excipients, potentially causing angioedema, bronchospasm, hypotension, or shock.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d26ce64e79b8c4a147140be2f9c63dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e50c145614f04be2319a31b74b80411</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786310048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Site Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection Site Reaction is a local adverse effect that can occur after the subcutaneous administration of insulin products. It has been reported with several insulin analogues, including Humalog®, Basaglar®, Lantus®, and Levemir®, and is observed more frequently in pediatric patients. In the case of Humalog, the reaction is classified as a common event, affecting roughly 1 % to 10 % of users.

Clinically, Injection Site Reaction manifests as a range of skin and soft‑tissue responses at the injection site. Typical signs and symptoms include pain or tenderness, erythema (redness), itching, urticaria (hives), swelling or edema, inflammation, bruising, and other localized edema‑type responses. These manifestations are generally confined to the area surrounding the insulin injection and are considered local rather than systemic adverse events.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d26ce64e79b8c4a147140be2f9c63dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d194d06ef99b3f8aa6b6542b7e93e762&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c580ebfc111aa29796af1a6f15941c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e50c145614f04be2319a31b74b80411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef15154a94043650617b3b24d8146a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318536</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
