<node id="Mild to Moderate Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Mild to Moderate Hypoglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mild to moderate hypoglycemia includes symptoms such as headache, blurred vision, sweating, dizziness, palpitations, tremor, hunger, and others.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786303800</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Severe Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Severe Hypoglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Severe Hypoglycemia is a life‑threatening medical condition characterized by an acute, markedly low blood‑glucose concentration that requires immediate external assistance. Clinical definitions commonly use plasma glucose thresholds ranging from below 36 mg/dL (2.0 mmol/L) to below 55.8 mg/dL (3.1 mmol/L) or below 50 mg/dL, each associated with central nervous system involvement. The hallmark manifestations include confusion, impaired cognition, seizures, loss of consciousness, and progression to coma; in the most extreme cases, irreversible neurological injury or death can occur. Because patients often lack awareness of the episode, prompt intervention by a third party—such as glucagon administration, intravenous glucose, or other emergency measures, sometimes followed by hospitalization—is essential.

The condition most frequently arises from iatrogenic factors, especially an insulin superdose or inappropriate intravenous insulin administration, but it can also occur with any excessive glucose‑lowering therapy, including intensive insulin regimens and multiple‑daily injections (MDI). Intensive insulin use, whether delivered by pen, pump, or other devices, is a recognized precipitant, and the risk can be mitigated by strategies such as insulin‑pump therapy, which has been shown in comparative studies to reduce the frequency of severe episodes relative to MDI. In addition, intermittently scanned continuous glucose monitoring (isCGM) systems are designed to detect rapid glucose declines and thereby help prevent severe hypoglycemia.

Severe Hypoglycemia is routinely recorded as a primary or composite safety outcome in clinical trials of insulin delivery methods and is classified as a serious adverse event. Reported incidences vary: for example, about 6 % of patients receiving insulin glargine (Lantus®) experienced an episode requiring third‑party assistance, while other studies observed no severe events in any treatment arm. Its prominence as a safety endpoint underscores its clinical importance in both routine diabetes management and investigational settings. Moreover, the condition is listed as an indication for SICI (Sustained‑Release Insulin Continuous infusion) therapy, reflecting its relevance in therapeutic decision‑making.

Overall, Severe Hypoglycemia denotes a dangerous drop in blood glucose that can occur in diabetes treatment, especially with intensive insulin use, and demands immediate attention and assistance to avert severe morbidity or mortality. Its measurement as an adverse outcome across numerous studies, its inclusion as an indication for specific insulin therapies, and the development of technologies such as insulin pumps and CGM systems all highlight the central role it plays in evaluating and improving diabetes care.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe Hypoglycemia is a dangerous low‑blood‑glucose condition that insulin infusion pumps aim to reduce the risk of.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe hypoglycemia refers to dangerously low blood glucose episodes, highlighted as a risk to avoid in HbA1c target recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe hypoglycemia is a dangerous low‑blood‑glucose event that may require treatment modification.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe hypoglycemia denotes serious low‑blood‑glucose episodes studied for health impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe Hypoglycemia is an extreme form of low blood glucose that can be dangerous.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-431b48980656b0322f263c358250b593&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4da80e99fd3e43cb7d3b260aca23fe56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb10c00517fa772be2ca9a42f497edf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecd2466ef4258dade38fbadd495188e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f079198593c1702b56062aa87e7801a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe094dd3d94334373a8497a7cb841e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9e5c50e8a808c537075377966ae2ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afc9722167670b0bfeef0b43f4b0df79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e9c7394777a2e452b57285541f75f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a592aedb257bad9532268b4056ec15c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d489de28e8fda3c3be3bb09ab29eb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d9c944d51a42525c017d9109f46684c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7223c781e745e72311fccfcdd58583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f85d0ab4cfadc905544cb9e30752dab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77c1608c27e3114f3a14f175efe260a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767d025776f0f59c05788e25616f412c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ace26fdb0244e3d0f714a007c9bbb51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b9f6a4a440ccb40e47c326ca6dd9346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36de8c99d97d234e12244c240c45f156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdb000b83632c89167783ad4e6f1fb64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7387e81b8141435df1372f5d8b27662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fba8ea0c6bdb8427804ba05f9fe49f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3b81193a603a098b1ff9f6f0f665fec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4af6b5c6f1fd844ec61928d1cce6ef9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a9b76fabae2f788f96d06b7d98c924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93594146e1ebe099cfc47906e940939f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d83801272f7a0ae0d797d60572fe578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998bee92df7a33cd9c3ddc60144e14f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aff91698679a52cd7b07563b7b2627a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-208cda7ee40e8d266fadd1eadff49e03</data>
