<node id="Superdose">
  <data key="d0">Superdose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Superdose is the term used to denote an excessively high amount of insulin—whether from any rapid‑acting formulation such as Humalog, APIDRA, APIDRA SOLOSTAR, or Fiasp—that exceeds the prescribed therapeutic dose and can precipitate severe, potentially life‑threatening hypoglycemia. The concept also appears as a dedicated “Superdose” section in the product‑specific labeling (the Bula) and in regulatory notifications, where it provides guidance on the consequences and management of an insulin overdose.

Clinically, a superdose‑induced hypoglycemic episode manifests rapidly with classic symptoms including sweating, tremor, palpitations, confusion, visual disturbances, seizures, and possible loss of consciousness. Because the decline in blood glucose can be swift, immediate recognition and treatment are essential. Standard emergency care involves rapid provision of fast‑acting carbohydrates (e.g., glucose tablets or juice) for conscious patients, and intravenous dextrose for those who are unconscious or unable to swallow. In more severe cases, glucagon injection may be required, followed by close monitoring of glucose levels and supportive care until stability is achieved.

Prevention centers on patient education and meticulous dosing practices. Healthcare providers are advised to emphasize accurate dose calculation, correct handling of insulin pens or vials, verification of insulin concentration, and proper injection technique. Adherence to prescribed schedules and regular review of dosing instructions help reduce the risk of an accidental superdose.

When a superdose occurs, the “Superdose” section of clinical guidelines and product labeling outlines a step‑by‑step approach: symptom recognition, immediate emergency treatment, and post‑event follow‑up to prevent recurrence. This unified information resource ensures clinicians and patients have clear, consistent directions for managing and avoiding the dangerous consequences of an insulin superdose.&lt;SEP&gt;Superdose refers to an excessive amount of insulin that can induce hypoglycemia with various symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Superdose refers to an excessive amount of Humulin R that can induce severe hypoglycemia with symptoms such as apathy, confusion, palpitations, sweating, vomiting, and headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e89f1dbae629e925f06732cebf41df16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9e5c50e8a808c537075377966ae2ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22df77f7bf817d736d90608e62a11d84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef35bd45e4ba8d20f4e9181011ccfcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca0b919135813e1d5b772face36989d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7223c781e745e72311fccfcdd58583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee5400065df79b02b6c04efc0f4854d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51761473ea6fe17b9e098a6bf98636b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b345bc26ec7f5d7c9db8c6004a50a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e7689ca0fc736d9eae94256f73fed52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53644541333e5ca7f2785d80235d27e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d5bb996e52cb71f15bc8c30d1b1d321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-922791f49d1bf928fe4f02ddd336927b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-831f4bbfc884fc8e9679630a819083e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d01ed6a801b11c1b53f4668059c1f9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813e7e990dba10e8d4147252f6547423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2714936869e55e840f5294caefeaee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-786395f3716fb61a79fd183c63e1a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7ca732691e1cbcafd65e378b8d80144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779af610d7298ce98490962cd90b4e76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ac99e7359716e2cecc2ec93159218b6</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
