<node id="Doping">
  <data key="d0">Doping</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Doping is the prohibited use of substances or methods that artificially enhance athletic performance and the violation of anti‑doping regulations that govern sport. It encompasses any performance‑enhancing drug or therapeutic agent whose presence in an athlete’s system may be deemed illegal by sport‑governing bodies because it can improve strength, endurance, recovery, or otherwise affect competition outcomes. When such a substance is detected in a doping control test, the athlete may face sanctions, including disqualification, suspension, or other penalties.

In the context of medical therapies, certain insulin analogues are specifically noted for their potential to trigger doping violations. Medications such as Humalog, Tresiba®, insulin glargine, Levemir® and the combination product APIDRA SOLOSTAR contain insulin or insulin‑like components that can produce performance‑enhancing effects, for example by improving glucose utilization and recovery. Pharmaceutical leaflets and related regulatory texts warn that the use of these insulin therapies may result in a positive doping test, indicating that they can be classified as prohibited substances under particular anti‑doping frameworks.

Thus, doping refers not only to the illicit use of traditional performance‑enhancing drugs but also to the possible classification of therapeutic medications—especially certain insulin formulations—as prohibited. Athletes using these medications must be aware of the risk of a positive test and may need to obtain a therapeutic use exemption or otherwise ensure compliance with the relevant anti‑doping rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Doping refers to the potential for the medication to cause doping violations.&lt;SEP&gt;Doping refers to the use of prohibited performance‑enhancing substances, mentioned as a possible effect of the medication.&lt;SEP&gt;Doping refers to the misuse of Humulin R for performance enhancement.&lt;SEP&gt;Doping refers to the misuse of substances like Humulin R to enhance performance, mentioned as a possible risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d26ce64e79b8c4a147140be2f9c63dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a976e1642df27c488174759a35ca23f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048d8fafb712e12cec45f2d3ec2c1e3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17844a4a12e6c05ba2d7b5f54b0f9bd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b40c2fbad301807d9646f27d94635cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ce520f0fe7dc4a6c79f92a25dfaff19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd94288932ad6675a1de2b46b3fd9128&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-518838d3c8e8110567b1891b704bfef9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93623071acdcd0ba060c8826ab5a2a81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4ba52cc293c5c002b470566321026d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c32255a7f064c18ff0b079798cfeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cedd8e88a8d35eade68ce02222b9e276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3237e202569462c758ec3d878c9faeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0851222a21a012cffce7a16710847208&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba6b31518be6fff1db8b68a28e82f200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fa819f7e1e42eb688d4894dc3ca7e64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577779892c0d463cd6bb1966b8a8ce2d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/Bula Humalog.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/LANTUS _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319724</data>
