<node id="Diabetic Ketoacidosis">
  <data key="d0">Diabetic Ketoacidosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) is a severe, acute metabolic crisis of diabetes that can be life‑threatening if not treated promptly. It is most commonly associated with type‑1 diabetes but may develop in any person with diabetes—including adults at diagnosis—when insulin availability is insufficient, such as after missed rapid‑acting doses, under‑dosing of long‑acting analogs, pump disconnection, or other situations that produce markedly elevated blood glucose. The condition is defined by a classic triad: profound hyperglycemia, elevated circulating ketone bodies, and metabolic acidosis reflected by low arterial pH and reduced serum bicarbonate, resulting in “high blood acids.” Stressful events, infections, equipment failure, or other precipitating factors can trigger DKA.

Clinically, DKA presents with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid (Kussmaul) breathing, and altered mental status; it may rapidly progress to coma, shock, or death. In children, DKA often appears as the first manifestation of type‑1 diabetes, while in adults it can arise at diagnosis or during periods of poor insulin adherence. The syndrome is routinely monitored in diabetes‑therapy studies as a serious adverse event, and epidemiological research—including the Umpierrez review—has compared its incidence across international pediatric cohorts. Screening programs at diabetes onset have been shown to reduce the frequency of DKA, underscoring the importance of early detection.

Management requires immediate intervention. Short‑acting intravenous insulin is administered to halt ketogenesis and lower glucose, while aggressive intravenous fluid replacement restores intravascular volume. Continuous monitoring and correction of electrolytes—particularly potassium—are essential, and clinicians must identify and treat any underlying precipitating cause such as infection or missed insulin doses. Prompt recognition and treatment are critical to prevent the severe outcomes associated with this diabetic emergency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67563459bb64c390a40207da52944afd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe094dd3d94334373a8497a7cb841e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bbae57922175913b7c36c594b4673fa2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2416b3e5e8ebc96cdb451a794fc78e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14772c7de5db762c492a75a1c744dae6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef15154a94043650617b3b24d8146a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f36263c657ad99a80e578ac49a333d5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a6f9e10c0d4af5fff0338eb861f9c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9b3836b3275f451c3acfb189f42a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf3629e41b33d8907b96157ddd2be394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b867cec4c5eed3b10e431cbd4078dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff2787d1cec4a771ee14b7d5d97324a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce776458e256df9d6070a419cab7449&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdb000b83632c89167783ad4e6f1fb64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05241912c4f8f5e79ae75071a1c7c604&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b03b7fdf0dd9c96dc459929ccccdccff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3b794ead59ea83de139af0358a0e7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f677e56841fc57bffd8bf5b6cc4a7797&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c5a5c97986d9b31b155b0c8f0cac1c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7387e81b8141435df1372f5d8b27662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fba8ea0c6bdb8427804ba05f9fe49f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06425d1f80d868bd58871b6a6e740102&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-511f6be03d2ea98970bd4c1b29b3e5c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4af6b5c6f1fd844ec61928d1cce6ef9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc0d4df5f59432ca990767b120f983a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cf24d486949a568d61c1de206cd3e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-343246c2eef6e8430b581340d6cd4762&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345ad64f464691cc28341ccd246032db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2cd796639bdb0e10eeda7ef3d9ebbce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-429fba9c3900b6d0b0c3ccfb38323e54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6601fea85bfbb9f20040c70b7e21695d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2752a78fbaafd94ca566ff099a4ba551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba9c9c7fe9dd4e901eb52088f3d59f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5ec01ed3c32ac663f3dc6cfa6fa09a3</data>
