<node id="Patients with Moderate and Severe Hepatic Dysfunction">
  <data key="d0">Patients with Moderate and Severe Hepatic Dysfunction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A patient group with reduced liver function in which pharmacokinetic parameters (AUC, Cmax, CL/F) of insulin aspart were found to be similar to those in patients with normal hepatic function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients with Normal Hepatic Function">
  <data key="d0">Patients with Normal Hepatic Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A patient group with normal liver function serving as the reference population for pharmacokinetic comparisons with hepatic‑dysfunction patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients with Moderate and Severe Renal Dysfunction">
  <data key="d0">Patients with Moderate and Severe Renal Dysfunction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A patient group with impaired kidney function (including severe alteration) in which creatinine clearance showed no apparent effect on insulin aspart’s AUC, Cmax, or CL/F.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients with Normal Renal Function">
  <data key="d0">Patients with Normal Renal Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A patient group with normal kidney function serving as the reference for renal‑dysfunction pharmacokinetic analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Creatinine Clearance">
  <data key="d0">Creatinine Clearance</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A laboratory measurement of renal function used to assess whether variations influence the pharmacokinetics of insulin aspart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AUC (Area Under Curve)">
  <data key="d0">AUC (Area Under Curve)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A pharmacokinetic parameter representing total drug exposure, reported as similar across hepatic and renal function groups for insulin aspart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cmax">
  <data key="d0">Cmax</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum observed concentration of insulin aspart after dosing, reported as comparable in patients with differing hepatic or renal function.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax represents the maximum plasma concentration of insulin after dosing; reported values differ between insulin aspart and regular insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax is the maximum plasma concentration of insulin, reported to differ between age groups in the Novolin® R study.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax is the maximum plasma concentration of insulin observed after administration, noted to differ between pediatric age groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax is the observed maximum plasma concentration of a drug after administration.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax is the maximum concentration of a drug in plasma, noted as differing between age groups in the pharmacokinetic profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Cmax is the maximum plasma concentration of insulin observed after dosing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b14c2f0985ae4a417c8fc99e339bd9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a6f9e10c0d4af5fff0338eb861f9c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9b3836b3275f451c3acfb189f42a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd40a6d69921cf300ed8eda043698d1a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318479</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CL/F (Clearance)">
  <data key="d0">CL/F (Clearance)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Apparent clearance of insulin aspart after oral bioavailability, shown to be similar regardless of hepatic or renal impairment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preclinical Safety Data">
  <data key="d0">Preclinical Safety Data</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Non‑clinical study results indicating no special danger for humans from insulin aspart, based on repeated‑dose toxicity, genotoxicity, and reproductive toxicity assessments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Receptor">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Receptor</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The cellular protein that binds insulin; in vitro tests showed insulin aspart dissociates from this receptor similarly to human insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin Receptor is the cellular receptor protein to which insulin and its analogues bind to exert metabolic effects.&lt;SEP&gt;The insulin receptor is a cellular protein that binds insulin, triggering downstream metabolic effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular receptor that insulin detemir binds with reduced affinity compared with human insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin Receptor is a cellular receptor that binds insulin molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin receptor is a cell‑surface protein that binds insulin to trigger glucose uptake in muscle and adipose cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02780a8bc1d372cc47e0ccf2eaab1a99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb9ea85b9ad8cd6940ae41a3ab5d1fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef15154a94043650617b3b24d8146a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f36263c657ad99a80e578ac49a333d5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2378c57a337d1ce7103c7086ebba76ac</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318542</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IGF-1 Receptor">
  <data key="d0">IGF-1 Receptor</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The insulin‑like growth factor‑1 receptor; insulin aspart displayed comparable binding dissociation to this receptor as human insulin in vitro.&lt;SEP&gt;Growth factor receptor that insulin detemir binds with reduced affinity compared with human insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;IGF-1 Receptor is a cellular receptor involved in growth signaling and can bind insulin detemir.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ef15154a94043650617b3b24d8146a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f36263c657ad99a80e578ac49a333d5b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786317552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypersensitivity to Insulin Aspart">
  <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity to Insulin Aspart</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An allergic reaction that contraindicates the use of insulin aspart or any of its excipients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Children Under One Year of Age">
  <data key="d0">Children Under One Year of Age</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A patient population for which insulin aspart is explicitly contraindicated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Basal‑Bolus Insulin Regimen">
  <data key="d0">Basal‑Bolus Insulin Regimen</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A dosing schedule combining long‑acting (basal) and rapid‑acting (bolus) insulin, where inappropriate timing with meals increases hypoglycemia risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concomitant Diseases (Infection, Fever, Renal, Hepatic, Adrenal, Pituitary, Thyroid)">
  <data key="d0">Concomitant Diseases (Infection, Fever, Renal, Hepatic, Adrenal, Pituitary, Thyroid)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Medical conditions that typically increase a patient’s insulin requirements, necessitating dose adjustments of insulin aspart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Transfer Between Types">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Transfer Between Types</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The clinical process of switching a patient from one insulin formulation or brand to another, requiring close medical supervision and possible dose modification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f895f97a023b65cced82334a7f1d1e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Dose Pharmacokinetic Study of Insulin Aspart">
  <data key="d0">Single-Dose Pharmacokinetic Study of Insulin Aspart</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A study in which a single dose of insulin aspart was administered to volunteers with varying renal function to assess pharmacokinetics.</data>
