<node id="Novolin® R">
  <data key="d0">Novolin® R</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Novolin® R is a brand of recombinant human insulin that is marketed primarily as a regular (short‑acting) insulin formulation for the treatment of diabetes mellitus. In clinical research it has frequently been employed as a comparator, notably against the rapid‑acting analogue NovoRapid in Study ANA‑2126 and other trials, where it serves to illustrate the pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic properties of a standard human insulin preparation.

The product is supplied as a clear, colorless aqueous solution in 10 mL glass vials or ampoules, and it is also available in cartridge formats for use with pen devices. The insulin solution is intended for subcutaneous injection, although it can be administered intravenously when rapid glucose control is required. The labeling emphasizes that Novolin® R is not designed for continuous infusion via insulin pumps. Detailed usage, storage, and safety instructions are provided in the professional package insert, which includes a comprehensive description of its absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination characteristics. Pediatric pharmacokinetic studies have been conducted, confirming its suitability for use in children under medical supervision.

Although most regulatory sources classify Novolin® R as a regular (short‑acting) human insulin, several descriptions in the literature refer to it as a “rapid‑acting” formulation. This discrepancy reflects differing terminology rather than a true difference in the product’s intrinsic action profile; the insulin’s onset of action is faster than intermediate‑acting NPH but slower than modern rapid‑acting analogues such as NovoRapid. Consequently, clinicians regard Novolin® R as a fast‑acting, short‑duration insulin that lowers blood glucose to help achieve normoglycemia after meals while being distinct from both intermediate‑acting and ultra‑rapid analogues.

In summary, Novolin® R is a human insulin product presented in vial or cartridge form, indicated for subcutaneous (and optionally intravenous) administration in patients with diabetes. It is clear and colorless, marketed in 10 mL containers, and is accompanied by a professional label that outlines its dosage, handling, and safety information. The formulation has been studied in both adult and pediatric populations and serves as a standard reference insulin in comparative clinical studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b4f2935f492529f0d551140b53a84fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecd2466ef4258dade38fbadd495188e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe094dd3d94334373a8497a7cb841e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68e2c07786710dd5250c6610f52b14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3396320470ee682ec5cc4f70ab001ef4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9b3836b3275f451c3acfb189f42a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6101c469482b48e341b4095a54907fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73559dc5d86ea52a348d603482e056e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df616353e032c899b02bfa4368bc2667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0851222a21a012cffce7a16710847208&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1124e7acf6619a1a7c60febd5211c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0689fd73d2061ee466b9b90e03bda141&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd40a6d69921cf300ed8eda043698d1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-786395f3716fb61a79fd183c63e1a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93f7f04b0529d6f0ace11829cc4fa3b4</data>
