</node>
<node id="Delayed Insulin Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Delayed Insulin Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Delayed Insulin Absorption is a potential effect of injecting insulin into skin areas affected by lipodystrophy or amyloidosis, leading to slower drug uptake.&lt;SEP&gt;Delayed Insulin Absorption denotes slower uptake of insulin from the injection site, potentially caused by tissue reactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6d0f18c80f8de2dee960a03599d12052&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318491</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poor Glycemic Control">
  <data key="d0">Poor Glycemic Control</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Poor Glycemic Control refers to suboptimal regulation of blood glucose levels, often resulting from improper insulin dosing or absorption issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6d0f18c80f8de2dee960a03599d12052</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Site Change">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site Change</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection Site Change refers to the practice of moving the location of subcutaneous insulin administration from one skin area to another, typically shifting from a region that has become affected—such as one showing lipohypertrophy, irritation, or other adverse reactions—to a previously unaffected area. This deliberate relocation is recommended as a management strategy after injection‑site complications arise, with the goal of preserving tissue health and maintaining consistent insulin absorption.

The change in injection site is recognized as a factor that can influence glucose control. Clinical observations and patient reports indicate that a sudden or abrupt shift in the injection location may precipitate hypoglycemia, likely because the new site can alter the rate and extent of insulin uptake. Consequently, healthcare providers advise that site rotations be performed methodically rather than abruptly, to minimize the risk of unexpected drops in blood glucose levels while still achieving the benefits of avoiding repeated trauma to a single area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6d0f18c80f8de2dee960a03599d12052&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d489de28e8fda3c3be3bb09ab29eb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca13e396b094f0c2f482892f220f538&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2416b3e5e8ebc96cdb451a794fc78e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a6f9e10c0d4af5fff0338eb861f9c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd396c45bc09fb646d156b4e69d328e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e622f0079074cd1916470223d22aa66</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Antidiabetic Medications">
  <data key="d0">Antidiabetic Medications</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Antidiabetic Medications are drugs used to manage diabetes, which may be adjusted following insulin dose changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6d0f18c80f8de2dee960a03599d12052</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/NOVORAPID _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786305922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weight Gain">
  <data key="d0">Weight Gain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Weight Gain is defined as an increase in body mass or body weight that is commonly evaluated as a safety outcome in clinical studies of diabetes treatments. It has been reported as a modest but measurable side effect of certain oral agents, particularly thiazolidinediones, where patients receiving these drugs may experience a noticeable rise in body weight.

In the context of insulin therapy, weight gain is also observed as an adverse outcome, though the magnitude varies with the specific insulin formulation and delivery method. Participants receiving insulin glargine (commercially known as Lantus®) have shown a modest increase in body weight, and similar modest gains have been documented in studies of insulin detemir, where the effect appears less pronounced. Comparative data indicate that weight gain is lower in patients treated with insulin Tecnósfera than in those using insulin Aspart, suggesting formulation‑dependent differences. Additionally, combined therapy that includes both thiazolidinediones and insulin has been associated with weight gain as an adverse effect.

Weight Gain has further been reported among patients using different insulin administration strategies, including single‑injection continuous infusion (SICI) and multiple‑daily‑injection (MDI) regimens. Across these modalities, the increase in body weight is generally characterized as modest and is routinely captured as a safety endpoint in insulin studies. Overall, weight gain remains a recognized, though variable, side effect of both certain oral diabetes agents and a range of insulin therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight gain is a potential side effect of insulin treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight gain is a possible side effect linked to subcutaneous insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight gain is identified as a factor that places individuals at higher risk, suggesting shorter screening intervals may be useful.</data>
