<node id="Diabetes Tipo 2">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Tipo 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Tipo 2 (type 2 diabetes) is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by persistently elevated blood‑glucose levels and marked insulin resistance. It is the most common form of diabetes mellitus and predominantly affects adult patients, often emerging in the context of obesity, advancing age, and other metabolic risk factors. Across epidemiological reports it is regularly cited as the prevalent adult‑onset diabetes, including in prevalence statistics for conditions such as lipodystrophy.

The pathophysiology of Diabetes Tipo 2 centers on a diminished cellular response to insulin combined with progressive β‑cell dysfunction. Insulin resistance is closely linked to excess adiposity, particularly visceral fat, and the condition frequently co‑exists with overweight or obesity, contributing to its high global burden. While the disease is chronic and currently lacks a definitive cure, its progression can be mitigated through lifestyle modification and pharmacotherapy.

Therapeutic regimens for Diabetes Tipo 2 commonly begin with lifestyle measures and oral antidiabetic agents, and many patients eventually require insulin therapy. When insulin is indicated, initial doses are generally lower than those used for type 1 diabetes, reflecting residual endogenous insulin secretion. Fully insulinized patients are advised to observe exercise safety measures to reduce hypoglycaemia risk. In addition to insulin, newer agents such as the GLP‑1 receptor agonist APIDRA are employed to improve glycaemic control and address weight‑related concerns.

Diabetes Tipo 2 serves as the central patient population in numerous clinical investigations. Phase III trials of insulin glargine (Lantus®) have enrolled adult type‑2 diabetes participants both as monotherapy and in combination with oral agents, evaluating efficacy, safety, and pharmacokinetic properties under the metabolic milieu of insulin resistance. Similar cohorts are used in studies of GLP‑1 agonists, where the emergence of new‑onset Diabetes Tipo 2 is often recorded as a secondary outcome, underscoring its relevance as a clinical endpoint. Pharmacokinetic assessments consistently emphasize how the disorder influences drug absorption, distribution, and action.

Overall, Diabetes Tipo 2 is depicted across the source material as a widespread, chronic, insulin‑resistant disease of adulthood, strongly associated with obesity and aging, managed primarily with insulin (at lower starting doses than type 1) and adjunctive agents, and serving as a key focus of contemporary therapeutic research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f079198593c1702b56062aa87e7801a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d87cbe9886e7203b6e7b26c85d60cb9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87576da8269707f5f8d929b71470e023&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b30ff3c650cf7cb71a3cf6e99f2c581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553e80da33ab48fb157ac9ff75436eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-228b9514d97e62787d4a60078f932ceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f46c503a378e6c69618036a038672417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65ee62ad12239ddc729c50a1aef854c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb620bf44b37187faceacd0405c4a819&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a4249ac93aec086cb333ed1507ea7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0689fd73d2061ee466b9b90e03bda141&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f8c8b2b40633cf37993b620002b889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f645bd7019e94151caaff47d6cbfcaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6df8d05eb3fdd356a1eaef16a26dd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72da6fb8b6a9ed059d146b43a3174f90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed325288512881a80fa42dba504c4bff</data>
