  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acetylsalicylic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Acetylsalicylic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) and related salicylates are mentioned as medications that can lower blood‑glucose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306291</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors">
  <data key="d0">Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of antidepressant medications that act by inhibiting the activity of the enzyme monoamine oxidase, thereby increasing the availability of monoamine neurotransmitters such as serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine in the central nervous system. They are prescribed primarily for the treatment of depressive disorders, especially when other antidepressant classes are ineffective or not tolerated.

In addition to their psychiatric effects, MAOIs have been observed to influence glucose metabolism and insulin dynamics. Clinical reports indicate that these drugs may lower blood‑glucose concentrations, which can reduce the amount of insulin required to achieve glycemic control. Consequently, MAOIs can diminish insulin efficacy and may potentiate insulin‑induced hypoglycemia, leading to a need for decreased insulin dosing. This effect has been documented in patients using various insulin formulations, including Humulin R, where MAOIs may specifically lower the insulin requirements. Healthcare providers therefore often monitor blood‑glucose levels closely and may adjust insulin regimens when a patient begins or discontinues MAOI therapy to avoid unintended hypoglycemic episodes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d96761d60c65f05f1530bc19b144cf1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ce520f0fe7dc4a6c79f92a25dfaff19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd94288932ad6675a1de2b46b3fd9128&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c32255a7f064c18ff0b079798cfeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fa819f7e1e42eb688d4894dc3ca7e64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577779892c0d463cd6bb1966b8a8ce2d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786319731</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Salbutamol">
  <data key="d0">Salbutamol</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Salbutamol is a β2‑adrenergic agonist bronchodilator that belongs to the class of sympathomimetic drugs. It is commonly cited as an example of a sympathomimetic agent used to relax airway smooth muscle in the treatment of asthma and other obstructive pulmonary conditions.  

In addition to its respiratory effects, Salbutamol has been reported to influence glucose metabolism. The drug can raise blood‑glucose levels by attenuating the glucose‑lowering action of insulin. Specifically, Salbutamol may diminish insulin‑mediated glucose reduction, reduce the hypoglycemic effect of insulin formulations such as insulin glargine, and lessen the efficacy of rapid‑acting insulin analogues like Apidra Solostar (insulin glulisine). Consequently, its use can lead to a decrease in insulin‑induced hypoglycemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d96761d60c65f05f1530bc19b144cf1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99841fd6d1e1fbf23f184e202bc760e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4ba52cc293c5c002b470566321026d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-465ad8b77c1c2a2a179062da20337ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b060d4f68031bd35dfafc19eadf1e9fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786315405</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terbutaline">
  <data key="d0">Terbutaline</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Terbutaline is a bronchodilator associated with higher blood‑glucose.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbutaline is a β2‑agonist noted as reducing insulin effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbutaline is a bronchodilator sympathomimetic that may decrease insulin efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbutaline is a β2‑agonist used for bronchospasm that can diminish insulin‑related hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbutaline is a sympathomimetic drug that reduces the insulin‑induced reduction of blood glucose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d96761d60c65f05f1530bc19b144cf1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786315471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Octreotide">
  <data key="d0">Octreotide</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Octreotide is a drug used for a rare condition involving excess growth hormone and can alter blood‑glucose.&lt;SEP&gt;Octreotide is a peptide drug that inhibits pancreatic function and can decrease insulin requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Octreotide is a medication that can decrease the insulin requirements of a patient.&lt;SEP&gt;A somatostatin analogue that can increase or decrease insulin requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Octreotide is a drug that may increase or decrease the insulin requirements of Levemir®.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ce520f0fe7dc4a6c79f92a25dfaff19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd94288932ad6675a1de2b46b3fd9128&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cedd8e88a8d35eade68ce02222b9e276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3237e202569462c758ec3d878c9faeae</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786317606</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lanreotide">
  <data key="d0">Lanreotide</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Lanreotide is similar to octreotide, used for excess growth hormone and may modify blood‑glucose.&lt;SEP&gt;A somatostatin analogue that can increase or decrease insulin requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Lanreotide is a drug that may increase or decrease the insulin requirements of Levemir®.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cedd8e88a8d35eade68ce02222b9e276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3237e202569462c758ec3d878c9faeae</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786317606</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thiazolidinediones">
  <data key="d0">Thiazolidinediones</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Thiazolidinediones are oral antidiabetic agents for type 2 diabetes that have been linked to heart‑failure when combined with insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Thiazolidinediones are a class of oral antidiabetic drugs that, when combined with insulin, have been associated with congestive heart failure.&lt;SEP&gt;Thiazolidinediones are a class of drugs used in liver and kidney transplant recipients, associated with fluid retention, heart failure, and osteopenia.&lt;SEP&gt;A class of oral antidiabetic drugs that improve insulin sensitivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acaa1440ae268669e1894588bb9e37c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c83accc14a3b482690949ff04716a0aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e7eef761aec994cc4558b2db5e1e08</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377257</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Food Intake">
  <data key="d0">Food Intake</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Food intake is the meal consumption that insulin dosing should be synchronized with.&lt;SEP&gt;Food intake describes the consumption of nutrients and calories, a variable examined in metabolic studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb7105c2156cb3f814ebb240d4a8a1fb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/LIPODISTROFIAS.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786321244</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machines">
  <data key="d0">Machines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Machines refer to tools and equipment whose safe operation may be compromised by hypo‑ or hyperglycemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306293</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acromegaly">
  <data key="d0">Acromegaly</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acromegaly is a rare condition of excess growth hormone treated with octreotide or lanreotide.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306293</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heart Failure">
  <data key="d0">Heart Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Heart failure is a cardiac condition that has been observed in patients using thiazolidinediones together with insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart failure is cardiac insufficiency that may be exacerbated by TZDs used with insulin in patients with existing heart disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart Failure is a cardiac condition whose risk increases when TZD is used together with insulin, especially in patients with underlying heart disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart failure is a cardiac condition for which SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 receptor agonists are advised due to cardiovascular benefit.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart Failure is a potential adverse outcome linked to thiazolidinedione therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4704ea4d5c15ebc8c043a077f05fd7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d489de28e8fda3c3be3bb09ab29eb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2416b3e5e8ebc96cdb451a794fc78e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ab5af7e42588297df4330ffb2c046b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e7eef761aec994cc4558b2db5e1e08</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/metas cuidado do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377340</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Novo Nordisk Farmacêutica do Brasil Ltda">
  <data key="d0">Novo Nordisk Farmacêutica do Brasil Ltda</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Novo Nordisk Farmacêutica do Brasil Ltda is the pharmaceutical company that manufactures Tresiba®.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306293</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oral Antidiabetic Drugs">
  <data key="d0">Oral Antidiabetic Drugs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Oral antidiabetic drugs are medications taken by mouth to control blood glucose, referenced as possible combination partners for Tresiba® in type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Oral antidiabetic drugs are medications taken by mouth to improve blood‑glucose control in diabetes type 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a20360c489112f7848983049bae7125</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306294</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GLP-1 Receptor Agonists">
  <data key="d0">GLP-1 Receptor Agonists</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">GLP‑1 receptor agonists are a class of drugs that mimic glucagon‑like peptide‑1, cited as an optional combination with Tresiba® for type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP‑1 receptor agonists are agents that can decrease insulin necessity.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP-1 receptor agonists are a class of drugs that includes liraglutida and act on the glucagon‑like peptide‑1 receptor.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP-1 Receptor Agonists are a class of oral agents that may be insufficiently controlling glucose in some T2D patients.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP-1 receptor agonists are a drug class (e.g., liraglutide, exenatide, semaglutide, tirzepatide) that affect gastric emptying but do not interfere with Afrezza absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP‑1 receptor agonists are a class of glucose‑lowering agents advised for patients with cardiovascular disease, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure due to proven benefit.&lt;SEP&gt;GLP-1 receptor agonists are a class of medications that improve glycemic control and provide cardiovascular benefit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c32255a7f064c18ff0b079798cfeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e7e47ca39ac4f4975da2b4b7e6d3c36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e6371d8083fb20b137912cdaeec1b8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8644d98286c4a296063176435b45d589&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ab5af7e42588297df4330ffb2c046b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aff91698679a52cd7b07563b7b2627a3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/metas cuidado do diabetes.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786372161</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rapid-Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Rapid-Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rapid‑Acting Insulin is a class of short‑duration insulin formulations that are absorbed rapidly after subcutaneous injection, beginning to lower blood glucose within minutes. Because of this fast onset, the medications are classified as rapid‑acting or fast‑acting insulins and are designed to provide immediate glucose regulation.

In clinical practice, rapid‑acting insulin is employed primarily to control post‑meal blood‑glucose excursions in patients with type 1 diabetes, often in combination with longer‑acting basal insulins such as Tresiba® to achieve comprehensive glycaemic control. Its quick action makes it suitable for use with syringes or pen devices when immediate insulin delivery is required.

Rapid‑acting insulin is also the preferred formulation for insulin pump therapy, where its swift pharmacodynamic profile allows pumps to deliver precise, minute‑by‑minute insulin doses that rapidly correct rising glucose levels. In the event of pump failure, the same rapid‑acting insulin can be administered manually with a syringe or pen to restore glucose regulation promptly.&lt;SEP&gt;Rapid‑acting insulin is a fast‑acting insulin taken before meals to control post‑meal glucose spikes.&lt;SEP&gt;Rapid‑acting insulin (RAA) refers to fast‑acting insulin formulations compared with inhaled Afrezza units.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of insulin delivered by pumps to quickly lower blood glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Rapid-Acting Insulin is a fast‑acting insulin formulation delivered by pumps or patch‑like devices for basal or bolus dosing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb0fb965e5ddd796999856ca376d4b9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1245bd9ff75c998b49dab861cc4cebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1700b8f7c792afbd394001e28bf632f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7715667f5fca178f0988984e932ecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e91ce385a4956221ce891caa7456655a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5913d17c337be62cd526d46fbdcd9a6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fba8ea0c6bdb8427804ba05f9fe49f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06425d1f80d868bd58871b6a6e740102&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c5a1107fcc9b632361d9c52064f45d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e6371d8083fb20b137912cdaeec1b8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cd925790899146eeb8a373879b5feac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_1778357531609.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786335885</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FlexTouch® System">
  <data key="d0">FlexTouch® System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The FlexTouch® system is a pre‑filled injection device containing 3 mL (300 U) of Tresiba® insulin and allowing up to 80 U per injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786306295</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Saccharomyces cerevisiae">
  <data key="d0">Saccharomyces cerevisiae</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Saccharomyces cerevisiae is a yeast strain employed to produce insulin degludeca by recombinant DNA technology.&lt;SEP&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae is a yeast employed as the expression host for recombinant production of insulin aspart.&lt;SEP&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae is the yeast strain employed as a biological factory for recombinant insulin production.&lt;SEP&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae is the yeast species used as the expression host for producing recombinant human insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae is a species of yeast employed as the host organism in recombinant DNA production of insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae is a yeast species used in recombinant DNA technology to produce human insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7e36686d077dcb6239405f60ea031&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb8954aaa1e1230598596ce6f5df249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d01ed6a801b11c1b53f4668059c1f9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7ca732691e1cbcafd65e378b8d80144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93f7f04b0529d6f0ace11829cc4fa3b4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786318426</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recombinant DNA Technology">
  <data key="d0">Recombinant DNA Technology</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Recombinant DNA technology is a biotechnological method used to generate insulin degludeca in yeast cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA technology is the method used to produce human analog insulin glargine in Escherichia coli.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA technology is the genetic engineering method employed to produce insulin glargine in Escherichia coli.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA technology is the method used to produce insulin detemir in Saccharomyces cerevisiae.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA Technology is a biotechnological method used to produce human insulin in yeast.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA technology refers to the genetic engineering method used to insert the human insulin gene into Escherichia coli.&lt;SEP&gt;Recombinant DNA Technology is the technique used to produce long‑acting insulin analogues such as Glargina U100 and Detemir.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e590341aac73dca916e560c2df9e60e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42b9c5d97c54f39fb69bb1169e1de4d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afc28422755c2c44f931bab7b6a2b477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a4249ac93aec086cb333ed1507ea7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d01ed6a801b11c1b53f4668059c1f9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1245bd9ff75c998b49dab861cc4cebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65cd86aee374fd1ea91bb4fd5ebee2a7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Insulinoterapia – Diretriz da Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes – Ed. 2024.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786331397</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
