<node id="Hipoglicemia Grave">
  <data key="d0">Hipoglicemia Grave</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hipoglicemia Grave is a clinical term for a severe episode of hypoglycemia in which blood‑glucose levels fall to a point that can lead to loss of consciousness, brain injury, or death.  In these situations the patient is unable to self‑treat and requires the immediate help of another person to receive rapid carbohydrate intake, glucagon injection, intravenous glucose, or other resuscitative measures.  The condition is therefore characterized not only by the depth of the glucose deficit but also by the necessity for external assistance to restore normal glycaemia.

In research and clinical practice, Hipoglicemia Grave is often identified using the International Society for Pediatric and Adolescent Diabetes (ISPAD) criteria for severe hypoglycemia.  A study that tracked such events reported incidence rates of 1.1 % in participants using the combined treatment FIASP® plus insulin degludec, 3.1 % with FIASP® administered post‑prandially plus insulin degludec, and 1.6 % when NovoRapid® was paired with insulin degludec.  These figures illustrate that the risk of Hipoglicemia Grave varies according to the insulin formulation and dosing regimen employed.

The risk of severe hypoglycemia is also heightened with certain administration routes of insulin analogues.  For example, the long‑acting insulin Levemir® (insulin detemir) has been associated with Hipoglicemia Grave when delivered intravenously, intramuscularly, or through continuous insulin infusion pumps, underscoring the importance of careful monitoring and dose adjustment in such delivery methods.

Overall, Hipoglicemia Grave denotes an extreme form of low blood‑sugar that is dangerous, may precipitate unconsciousness or fatal outcomes, and invariably demands prompt external intervention—typically via carbohydrate ingestion, glucagon administration, or intravenous glucose—to prevent serious neurological damage or death.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia Grave denotes severe hypoglycemic episodes, the primary safety outcome reduced by rapid‑acting insulin analogues.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia grave is severe low blood glucose, noted as being avoided with CGM use.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia grave is a severe form of low blood glucose that requires more cautious treatment targets.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia grave is severe low blood glucose, defined as Glicemia below 54 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Severe hypoglycemia influencing target decisions and associated with study findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11cce86325de9748e794648198242578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553e80da33ab48fb157ac9ff75436eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a70857656e32cc73aaa270a75eb33af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2073bddd90c7dd8e156b67821f0f3247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49c8461f43dc12d7df9c921efeb0465&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d01ed6a801b11c1b53f4668059c1f9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-929e31613caadc967412041c8c2155b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb0cd81f0439cbb74e3c0ff54236c45d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfa23fc15cc4d520a32488843dbd710b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c81e7ab8160456d9a63a9e0868205b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276b766b8d515b1e7f388b5b7381bfbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e0d8e22a06c73855abed11561ce467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12999315830b302d973dd1908eab1e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0cc9dbb0b2eb4685ac17cfe445b7ce7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/TRESIBA _ BulasMed.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Insulinoterapia – Diretriz da Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes – Ed. 2024.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/cgm.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
