<node id="Protease Inhibitors">
  <data key="d0">Protease Inhibitors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Protease Inhibitors are a class of antiretroviral medications that act by blocking the activity of viral protease enzymes, thereby preventing the maturation of infectious HIV particles. They are listed among the major drug types used to treat HIV infection and are commonly referenced simply as protease inhibitors or PIs.

In addition to their antiviral action, protease inhibitors are associated with several metabolic disturbances. Clinical observations indicate that they can impair insulin’s ability to lower blood glucose, leading to reduced insulin effectiveness or efficacy. This insulin resistance is linked to pancreatic β‑cell apoptosis and may blunt the glucose‑lowering response that insulin normally produces, which can diminish the occurrence of insulin‑induced hypoglycemia but also compromise overall glycemic control. Protease inhibitors are also known to contribute to lipodystrophy through multiple metabolic pathways, further aggravating insulin resistance and altering body‑fat distribution.

Because of their impact on glucose metabolism, protease inhibitors can interfere with the reliability of glycated‑hemoglobin (A1C) measurements, making A1C values less dependable for monitoring long‑term blood‑sugar control in patients receiving these drugs. Consequently, clinicians often need to consider alternative or additional methods for assessing glycemic status when patients are treated with protease inhibitors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d96761d60c65f05f1530bc19b144cf1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65840dd2cbd5c9984dfc4334378df5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-465ad8b77c1c2a2a179062da20337ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86b5a9c60ab13c2016dd454edf634c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d01033177672e6c36e0a368db88e586d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55e58732610596ce9a8f3145d34f75a7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/LIPODISTROFIAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377340</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Atypical Antipsychotics">
  <data key="d0">Atypical Antipsychotics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Atypical antipsychotics (e.g., olanzapine, clozapine) can decrease insulin‑mediated glucose reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Atypical antipsychotics are a class of psychiatric medications that can reduce insulin’s glucose‑lowering effect.&lt;SEP&gt;Atypical Antipsychotics are a class of antipsychotic medications, exemplified by olanzapina and clozapina.&lt;SEP&gt;Atypical antipsychotics are drugs that reduce the insulin‑induced reduction of blood glucose.&lt;SEP&gt;Atypical antipsychotics are medications that may precipitate diabetic ketoacidosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Atypical antipsychotics are a class of drugs linked to higher diabetes risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db0edd23b6b502ccbbf62bfd601ce68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d96761d60c65f05f1530bc19b144cf1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-465ad8b77c1c2a2a179062da20337ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f09811b149a29105854a0b1e84951e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55e58732610596ce9a8f3145d34f75a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a111d4b40ec241cf54126f594a80308</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377372</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
