<node id="A1C">
  <data key="d0">A1C</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A1C, also known as hemoglobin A1c or HbA1c, is a laboratory measurement of glycated hemoglobin that quantifies the proportion of hemoglobin molecules chemically linked to glucose. By reflecting the average concentration of glucose in the bloodstream over the preceding two to three months (approximately 8–12 weeks), A1C provides a integrated view of long‑term glycemic exposure rather than moment‑to‑moment fluctuations. Results are expressed either as a percentage of total hemoglobin or, in regions that use the IFCC standard, in millimoles per mole (mmol/mol).

The test is widely employed in clinical practice and research for several purposes. It serves as a primary diagnostic tool for diabetes and pre‑diabetes, with commonly cited thresholds such as 5.7 % (39 mmol/mol) for early abnormal glucose metabolism, 5.9 % for heightened risk, and 6.5 % (48 mmol/mol) for diabetes diagnosis. Percentages between 5.5 % and 6.5 % are linked to varying degrees of diabetes risk, while values between 5.6 % and 7.6 % are frequently reported in clinical studies. A1C also aids in identifying individuals at risk for specific conditions such as post‑transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM) and cystic fibrosis‑related diabetes (CFRD). In many adult and pediatric trials, changes in A1C constitute the primary efficacy endpoint, allowing investigators to compare baseline and follow‑up values and to assess the impact of interventions such as insulin pump therapy, flash continuous glucose monitoring (CGM), and other glucose‑monitoring technologies.

Therapeutically, A1C guides treatment decisions and helps set individualized glycemic targets; a common goal for many adults is an A1C below 7.0 % (53 mmol/mol). Reductions in A1C observed in trials, including the APIDRA study, are interpreted as evidence of improved long‑term glucose control and are often correlated with other performance metrics like time‑in‑range derived from CGM data. Higher A1C levels are associated with a disproportionate increase in diabetes risk and with greater variability in glucose control.

A1C testing is low‑cost, can be added to routine prenatal laboratory panels without requiring fasting, and offers a convenient snapshot of chronic hyperglycemia. However, its accuracy may be compromised in conditions that alter red blood cell turnover, such as anemia, hemoglobinopathies, or other hematologic disorders, which can lead to misleading results. Despite these limitations, A1C remains a standardized, widely accepted biomarker for evaluating and managing chronic glucose dysregulation.</data>
