<node id="Hiperglucemia">
  <data key="d0">Hiperglucemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hiperglucemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high concentrations of glucose in the bloodstream. It is defined as a state of elevated blood‑glucose levels, particularly when values exceed 250 mg/dL, and is synonymous with hyperglycemia.  

The primary cause of hiperglucemia is insufficient insulin availability, which may result from inadequate insulin infusion, delayed or reduced insulin delivery, or an early insulin action that fails to match carbohydrate intake. Additional contributors can include physical activity, other physiological stressors, or factors that interfere with normal insulin signaling.  

Clinically, hiperglucemia is identified by glucose readings above the specified threshold and prompts specific therapeutic actions. Treatment protocols commonly prescribe the administration of a correction bolus of insulin to bring glucose levels back into target range. When insulin is delivered via an insulin pump, the frequency of high‑glucose episodes tends to decrease, reflecting the benefit of continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion.  

Overall, hiperglucemia is addressed through a combination of monitoring, threshold‑based decision rules, and corrective insulin dosing, with detailed troubleshooting steps provided in clinical guidelines to prevent and manage episodes of excessively high blood glucose.&lt;SEP&gt;Condition of high blood glucose that can result from insufficient insulin infusion.&lt;SEP&gt;Hiperglucemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high blood glucose levels, mentioned as a potential risk when the pump is disconnected for prolonged periods.&lt;SEP&gt;Hiperglucemia is a medical condition defined as glucose sensor readings above the target maximum or above 250 mg/dL, with associated symptoms noted.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f5ccf80661c0e52c3a3465ab87c6343&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5863810bebfd1be64b8af80afcd57e11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9688a6d3c7f751ad34af5a1cde24fa2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-644dc086af7b196cc5bc332524608d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a717b5a81dbba0bd3cf2aae23dc0e309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beee0553403f566be565a996251cac46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa7ad1de214645f0e643708dc1efab8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34da912924aa29d035c8cd1c3f4f6ae3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c123ae434326de3ccfe03d252e5d267&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1255cfcf42afb2c6045de0d45b11e99d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-646b0d64191b72fd929f7fb514a8cb8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0a4d039b786601ed8a06d8adad50938&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f369332c7e5ee7ed284fec07f68d394d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-505b4450257d1a728c397959b044ee97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ed0e4c13d1643a77d1d74092a8cda5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b29e7a7839d7ff2b29e72fb4912618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817893fc39ccf7551d476cefaf7f32ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eaa74c3ef8165f52ddf18e17a240a9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbaec668e54aa5cf462a7bd08d7813c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-381370d223aabc9b355b55a05c98e07b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8328f990d0902170699f76dac78b01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e303ffa37ca96974ad7399e052659bb</data>
