<node id="American Diabetes Association">
  <data key="d0">American Diabetes Association</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">American Diabetes Association (ADA) is a U.S.-based national professional organization that serves as a leading authority on diabetes prevention, treatment, research, and management. It is best known for publishing the annually updated “Standards of Medical Care in Diabetes,” a comprehensive set of clinical standards, practice guidelines, and specific glycemic targets such as an HbA1c of less than 7 percent, pre‑meal glucose ranges, and recommendations for daily glucose monitoring. These standards also define the classification and diagnosis of diabetes, provide criteria for older‑adult care, and outline the management of hypoglycemia and hyperglycemic crises. The ADA’s Professional Practice Committee coordinates the creation of numerous position statements, consensus reports, and specialized guidelines, including joint statements on intensive glycemic control and cardiovascular outcomes, reports on insulin pump therapy, automated insulin delivery systems, digital diabetes‑app technology, and hyperglycemia management. The organization collaborates with international bodies such as the International Society for Pediatric and Adolescent Diabetes (ISPAD) and the Swedish Diabetes Association, and it contributed to establishing the correlation between A1C and mean glucose levels.

In addition to guideline development, the ADA provides a broad array of educational materials and an online resource platform for patients, health‑care professionals, and the public. It publishes patient‑focused pages on the signs, symptoms, and treatment of hypoglycemia, including the widely referenced “15‑15 Rule” for rapid glucose correction. The association also offers tools such as the ADA Diabetes Risk Test and a 1‑800‑DIABETES hotline to support early detection and self‑management. Its resources extend to specific populations, exemplified by the youth‑onset type 2 diabetes position statement, the recommendation to use IADPSG diagnostic criteria for gestational diabetes mellitus, and contributions to cystic fibrosis‑related diabetes (CFRD) clinical care guidelines. The ADA co‑authored scientific statements on staging presymptomatic type 1 diabetes and participated in consensus reports on precision medicine, and it published a book on atypical diabetes.

Research funding and advocacy are core components of the ADA’s mission. The organization actively finances diabetes research, advocates for policy changes that improve care and access, and partners with other professional societies to advance diabetes science. Its publications, including the seminal “Diagnosis and classification of diabetes mellitus,” are frequently cited in scholarly literature on hypoglycemia, diabetes classification, and related topics, underscoring the ADA’s influence in both clinical practice and academic research. Through guideline development, educational outreach, research support, and public‑health advocacy, the American Diabetes Association remains a central hub for clinical guidance, patient resources, and professional collaboration within the global diabetes community.</data>
