<node id="BMJ">
  <data key="d0">BMJ</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">BMJ, formally known as the British Medical Journal, is a leading, peer‑reviewed, general‑medical journal that publishes original clinical research, systematic reviews, meta‑analyses, and cohort studies across a broad range of health disciplines. It is widely cited for its rigorous editorial standards and its role in disseminating evidence that informs clinical practice worldwide.

The journal has featured numerous studies related to diabetes. Notably, BMJ published multiple 2007‑2008 investigations on self‑monitoring of blood glucose, comparative evaluations of diagnostic tests for diabetes, and the DECODE Study Group article that introduced new diagnostic criteria for the disease. It also served as the outlet for the United Kingdom Prospective Diabetes Study (UKPDS) 35 findings and a 2019 cohort study examining diabetes outcomes.

In addition to primary research, BMJ has contributed to the evidence base on diabetes prevention and management. It released a study proposing benefit‑based, tailored treatment strategies to improve diabetes prevention, as well as a 2010 systematic review and meta‑analysis assessing the effects of treatment in women with gestational diabetes mellitus. These publications underscore BMJ’s commitment to advancing both general and specialty medical knowledge.</data>
