<node id="Multiple Daily Injections">
  <data key="d0">Multiple Daily Injections</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Multiple Daily Injections (MDI) is an insulin‑delivery regimen in which a person with diabetes administers several separate insulin doses each day, typically using syringes, pens, or similar injection devices. The regimen involves multiple injections per day to cover basal (background) insulin needs and bolus (mealtime) requirements, and it is commonly employed as the standard comparator in clinical research that evaluates alternative delivery methods such as continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (insulin pump therapy).  

MDI has been referenced as the comparator arm in a variety of studies, including the STAT trial, the INHALE‑3 trial, and other real‑world investigations that contrast multiple daily injections with continuous insulin infusion. These studies consistently describe MDI as the conventional approach against which the efficacy, safety, and patient‑reported outcomes of pump therapy are measured.  

Overall, Multiple Daily Injections represent the traditional, multi‑dose insulin administration strategy used worldwide for both type 1 and type 2 diabetes management, serving as a benchmark in research and clinical practice for evaluating newer insulin delivery technologies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd45744c4c50bb97abce1352018de47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a76447c936e157673f084ffb74520d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca8620e735c40bfe850134ccea9a7154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0e51dec97ab970bbc2f21878a8735ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00f6c2252dc945e977b3a2b2f495ffe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bc5d11c9f848439320623cad3d3a38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4b76ae88b212b3a0a4d05c250e05e0c</data>
