<node id="DM1">
  <data key="d0">DM1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DM1 denotes diabetes mellitus type 1, a chronic autoimmune form of diabetes characterized by the absolute loss of pancreatic β‑cell function and consequently an inability to produce endogenous insulin. Because insulin production is essentially absent, individuals with DM1 require lifelong insulin therapy to maintain glycaemic control. The condition is universally recognized in clinical practice, guideline documents and research literature as the insulin‑deficient diabetes population.

In guideline contexts, DM1 is the specific patient subgroup addressed by recommendations such as those from the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE), which compare sensor‑augmented insulin pump therapy (SICI) with multiple‑daily injections (MDI) for managing the disease. The entity is also linked to therapeutic targets, including recommended HbA1c levels, risk‑reduction outcomes from intensive glucose‑control studies, and hypoglycaemia time‑in‑range goals that inform both treatment decisions and outcome measurement for people with type 1 diabetes.

DM1 appears repeatedly in clinical research. It is the population examined in the SICI indication framework, the REPLACE‑BG trial that evaluated continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) use, and the Conceptt Study, which investigated therapeutic strategies for autoimmune diabetes. These studies have explored the impact of various insulin delivery methods, CGM technologies, and intensive glycaemic control on complications and quality of life for patients with DM1.

Additional descriptions of DM1 note its relevance in studies of transient albuminúria, underscoring the broader metabolic and renal implications of the disease. Across all sources, DM1 consistently refers to type 1 diabetes mellitus, emphasizing its autoimmune nature, the necessity for insulin replacement, and its central role in guideline‑driven and investigational contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;DM1 denotes type 1 diabetes mellitus, an autoimmune form of diabetes characterized by loss of insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;DM1 refers to Diabetes Mellitus type 1, classified when autoantibodies are positive.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7387e81b8141435df1372f5d8b27662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fba8ea0c6bdb8427804ba05f9fe49f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-336a2f2c9b510e23c35be95e2ff7a72a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2d6e4597425d2a83c3efcd9ca07f65c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91a09040fc4581d7aad2216dba9fba79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276b766b8d515b1e7f388b5b7381bfbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e484a5a1df875be2e1525c8c64e2007&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f716359c14483f9be36a73861d75008&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dee0a79e4583f41e757f9a20f56f397&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f9624abde6cf128707fcc0466a4c9a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d35e6caf612b4c4984ade9315ebc7431&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad23b9b7ef0802d02c046415e54e3fcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078f5907e86c2158587c79b26b0cd683&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e551f4b7f03919afc1d663b3b2a2efa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-858cd6ea7fba353827f5ab95be1157ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fee32f1fb0c848b0799071a86119b142</data>
