<node id="Diabetes Control and Complications Trial">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Control and Complications Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Diabetes Control and Complications Trial (DCCT) was a large, landmark clinical study that examined the effects of different insulin‑treatment strategies on patients with insulin‑dependent (type 1) diabetes. Conducted as a randomized comparison of intensive insulin therapy versus conventional therapy, the trial was designed to determine whether tighter glycemic control could reduce the incidence of long‑term diabetes complications.

The DCCT’s primary focus was on evaluating the impact of intensive treatment on the development of microvascular complications such as retinopathy, nephropathy, and neuropathy, while also monitoring the frequency and severity of hypoglycemic episodes. The study demonstrated that intensive insulin management significantly lowered the risk of these complications, although it also highlighted an increased occurrence of severe hypoglycemia in the intensively treated group.

In addition to its clinical findings, the DCCT played a pivotal role in the standardization of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) measurement. The trial validated standardized HbA1c assay methods and has since been cited as a reference point for calibrating A1C testing in both research and clinical practice. Its data have been frequently referenced in subsequent investigations of plasma glucose, HbA1c, and related metabolic outcomes, establishing the DCCT as a foundational benchmark for diabetes research and treatment guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-126f927723331d6ce149cf77ed397bbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-494fef0b8efc2c44b3b49ebf9d74b7f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4af6b5c6f1fd844ec61928d1cce6ef9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4c458a11c8c36c5c212c3aa69b026c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dec009976a44828d0e619e7fa5b1c5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2faebf8275a86cd8d9995c769b0926a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-664916c2444f157f5d7d9bc41b032d26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44888abd4ca8a1953f751ed6c41922e6</data>
