<node id="JAMA">
  <data key="d0">JAMA</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">JAMA, the Journal of the American Medical Association, is a major, leading, peer‑reviewed medical journal that publishes original research, reviews, and commentary across the full spectrum of clinical medicine. It is widely regarded as one of the most influential medical publications in the United States and is frequently cited in reference lists for its high‑impact articles.

As a peer‑reviewed outlet, JAMA serves as a primary source for clinicians, researchers, and policymakers seeking rigorously vetted evidence. Its articles are routinely referenced in studies on diverse topics such as islet autoantibodies and the risk of progression to diabetes in children, underscoring its role in disseminating critical findings that shape clinical practice and public health guidelines.

JAMA has published a variety of notable investigations, including a reply that examined complications of insulin pump therapy versus injection therapy, the large‑scale Emerging Risk Factors Collaboration study, the DIAMOND trial, and a 2009 article linking hypoglycaemic episodes to an increased risk of dementia. These examples illustrate the journal’s breadth in covering both therapeutic comparisons and epidemiological research that influence patient care and medical knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;JAMA is a medical journal that published the study on seroconversion to multiple islet autoantibodies.\&lt;SEP&gt;JAMA is a medical journal cited for research on diabetic ketoacidosis incidence.&lt;SEP&gt;Journal that published the sickle cell trait and A1c study.&lt;SEP&gt;The Journal of the American Medical Association that published prevalence data from the SEARCH for Diabetes in Youth Study and the Fr1da screening results.&lt;SEP&gt;JAMA is a major medical journal publishing epidemiological studies, such as diabetes prevalence analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;The Journal of the American Medical Association published research linking gestational diabetes to maternal disorders and childhood adiposity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-126f927723331d6ce149cf77ed397bbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f35eed8b49aa0b698012777f6ec96ce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b7df11ccfb12f819e6f60481970587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc0d4df5f59432ca990767b120f983a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddc3658b93eb925d28d625c4e2ce652f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e668c40e3bc21d982ed03a052f0f9339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-664916c2444f157f5d7d9bc41b032d26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acba683ee0809d1c3f6e35c228f485ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-703ccc96df3edd610232f0cca4bf7afa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8df31d65b128b9d70b82bb8af257332&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c122c1c6d7dd36110b6ddba2c074d122&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba9c9c7fe9dd4e901eb52088f3d59f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab98b1c07f7bbaa3ad28fb83167f489&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a15ed0aa8749d4f76e8d725e8752873&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fedb8cdc485be3c8e38133ddb9ea67cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84032013252fd71963ed381e09a3116d</data>
