<node id="Glycemic Targets">
  <data key="d0">Glycemic Targets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Glycemic Targets are the specific blood‑glucose and hemoglobin A1c (A1C) goals recommended for the management of diabetes.  In the United States, the American Diabetes Association (ADA) defines these targets as the desired ranges of fasting, pre‑meal, and post‑prandial glucose levels, as well as overall A1C percentages, that patients should aim to achieve and maintain.  The targets serve as benchmarks for clinicians and individuals with diabetes to guide therapeutic decisions, including medication dosing such as the rapid‑acting inhaled insulin Afrezza.

The recommended glucose ranges are individualized according to patient characteristics, disease severity, comorbidities, and risk of hypoglycemia.  For many adults, a stringent A1C goal of less than 6.5 % may be appropriate, whereas a less stringent goal of around 7 % is often advised for older adults, those with longer disease duration, or patients with significant cardiovascular or renal disease.  Corresponding blood‑glucose targets typically span fasting values of 80–130 mg/dL and post‑prandial values below 180 mg/dL, but clinicians may adjust these limits upward to avoid hypoglycemia in vulnerable individuals.

In acute‑illness or hospital settings, glycemic targets become short‑term objectives that must be achieved and sustained to reduce complications.  These in‑hospital goals are often tighter for critically ill patients but still balance the need to prevent both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia.  Additionally, glycemic targets are referenced in protocols for the rapid, short‑term relaxation of hypoglycemia, providing clinicians with reference ranges to safely bring glucose levels back into the desired therapeutic window.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycemic targets are clinical goals for blood glucose levels, often assessed using A1C measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08f12a2de420625ccc88f737d06b7e84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c5a1107fcc9b632361d9c52064f45d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fe769b03fc66d4a691f3b7f741b92cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-511f6be03d2ea98970bd4c1b29b3e5c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4b76ae88b212b3a0a4d05c250e05e0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345ad64f464691cc28341ccd246032db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ab5af7e42588297df4330ffb2c046b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36ddeaa53bc38bee375d7ac6d71485d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aff91698679a52cd7b07563b7b2627a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44888abd4ca8a1953f751ed6c41922e6</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/metas cuidado do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
