  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dose Conversion Factor">
  <data key="d0">Dose Conversion Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A factor used to convert inhaled insulin dose to subcutaneous insulin equivalents, noted as conservative in the Galderisi Study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e76c949a607724dd052d9fc4c7127a18</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786332842</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Insulin Delivery (AID) Systems">
  <data key="d0">Automated Insulin Delivery (AID) Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Automated Insulin Delivery (AID) Systems are technology platforms that provide continuous insulin dosing, improving overall glycemia and time-in-range for individuals with insulin‑treated diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed‑loop insulin pump systems that automatically adjust basal and bolus insulin, typically relying on subcutaneous RAA insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;AID systems are automated insulin delivery technologies evaluated in randomized trials and real‑world evidence for improving glycemic control.&lt;SEP&gt;Automated Insulin Delivery (AID) systems are devices that combine insulin pumps and glucose monitoring to manage diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Automated Insulin Delivery (AID) Systems are digitally connected insulin pumps that can receive glucose data from CGM devices to adjust dosing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c9d162accf67a3761df6b1c9113e193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-127dc0e8b8b960bfd1fce3df4f590120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2767372b4b5395439f542016058306a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4daf1d7e9da44e316a5b4dd21bc2dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fe1eff7b58582dec4c8ae7578e20e66</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786335978</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TI">
  <data key="d0">TI</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">TI is the abbreviation for Technosphere Insulin, the insulin powder component of the Afrezza inhaled insulin system.&lt;SEP&gt;TI is the dry‑powder formulation of recombinant human insulin used in the Afrezza delivery system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c9d162accf67a3761df6b1c9113e193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbb54288f56e407604ea361f58e58233</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786333261</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type 1 Diabetes (T1D)">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 Diabetes (T1D)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 Diabetes (T1D) is an autoimmune condition requiring insulin therapy, for which Automated Insulin Delivery systems were originally designed.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 Diabetes is a diabetes subtype referenced as a patient population in clinical investigations of AID systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c9d162accf67a3761df6b1c9113e193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4daf1d7e9da44e316a5b4dd21bc2dee</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786332842</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type 2 Diabetes (T2D)">
  <data key="d0">Type 2 Diabetes (T2D)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 2 Diabetes (T2D) is a metabolic disorder that may be managed with insulin, and Automated Insulin Delivery systems have demonstrated benefit for insulin‑treated patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 Diabetes, specifically insulin‑treated cases, is mentioned as a patient population in studies of AID systems and inhaled insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c9d162accf67a3761df6b1c9113e193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4daf1d7e9da44e316a5b4dd21bc2dee</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786332842</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eda Cengiz">
  <data key="d0">Eda Cengiz</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Eda Cengiz, MD, MHS, is a co‑author of the article and is affiliated with the University of California, San Francisco.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c9d162accf67a3761df6b1c9113e193</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/cengiz-beck-2026-use-of-inhaled-insulin-with-automated-insulin-delivery-systems.pdf</data>
