<node id="Food and Drug Administration">
  <data key="d0">Food and Drug Administration</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Food and Drug Administration is the U.S. regulatory agency that approved the Dexcom G6 system in 2018.&lt;SEP&gt;U.S. regulatory agency that approves medical devices and software for safety and efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;U.S. regulatory agency that exercised enforcement discretion to permit CGM device use in hospitals during the pandemic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fdfdc209a5e3bb46f199c55baac15b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c93787ffd2c022283fc0663decb1a184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fe769b03fc66d4a691f3b7f741b92cb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/monitoramento contínuo de glicose.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1786335929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SMCG">
  <data key="d0">SMCG</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SMCG refers to the use of continuous glucose monitoring technology to adjust glucose levels in people with diabetes, enhancing glycemic control and lowering hypoglycemia risk.&lt;SEP&gt;SMCG is the method of continuous glucose monitoring using sensors attached to the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Sistema de Monitorização Contínua de Glicose, a minimally invasive system that records interstitial glucose levels via a subcutaneous sensor, transmitter, and receiver.&lt;SEP&gt;SMCG is a sensor that measures glucose in interstitial fluid for long‑term glycemic control.&lt;SEP&gt;SMCG refers to subcutaneous glucose monitoring sensors that may display real-time readings or collect data for later analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fdfdc209a5e3bb46f199c55baac15b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79d0e4d0f656bd87d2d0c4657378db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e26d518805695de3e4f9d41ae1d3e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ac99b8588a8c480f44a5519509f7713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f716359c14483f9be36a73861d75008</data>
