<edge source="Insulina" target="Hipoglicemia">
  <data key="d7">15.0</data>
  <data key="d8">(Hipoglicemia, Insulina) describes the well‑documented adverse relationship in which the administration of insulin—whether as standard therapy, an excessive dose, or an overdose—can precipitate episodes of hypoglycemia. Hypoglycemia is reported as the most common and frequent adverse reaction to all insulin preparations, including specific products such as Humalog Mix 25 KwikPen. The risk is heightened when insulin is injected incorrectly; for example, delivering the drug into muscle tissue rather than the intended subcutaneous layer can increase the likelihood of low blood‑glucose events. Likewise, errors in dosing magnitude, timing of injections, or overall administration technique are recognized contributors to hypoglycemia. In summary, any improper insulin use—whether due to excessive dosage, inappropriate injection site, or mistimed administration—poses a significant risk of inducing hypoglycemic episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Excessive insulin can precipitate hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Hipoglicemia acts as a barrier to insulin treatment adherence.&lt;SEP&gt;Excess insulin can precipitate hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Excessive insulin dose can cause hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Use of Insulina can lead to Hipoglicemia if dosage is excessive or timing is off.</data>
  <data key="d9">adverse effect,adverse reaction,barrier,causation,causative factor,cause,common side effect,dose-related risk,dosing,excess risk,low blood sugar,overdose,potential side effect,risk,risk factor,safety,side effect</data>
