<edge source="Hipoglicemia" target="Insulina Glargina">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hipoglicemia ↔ Insulina Glargina refers to the occurrence of hypoglycemia that can be associated with the use of insulin glargine (Insulina Glargina). Throughout clinical experience and research, hypoglycemia is recognized as a possible adverse effect of this long‑acting insulin formulation, especially when dosing is not appropriately calibrated.

Improper dosing or failure to adjust the dose to changing physiological needs can precipitate hypoglycemia during insulin glargine therapy. Because insulin glargine has a prolonged subcutaneous action, its glucose‑lowering effect persists for many hours, which not only raises the risk of hypoglycemia if excess insulin is administered but can also delay recovery once low blood glucose occurs. Consequently, careful dose titration and patient education are essential to mitigate this risk.

A particular period of heightened vulnerability is the postpartum phase. After delivery, insulin requirements drop rapidly, and a swift reduction in insulin glargine dose may be necessary. If the dose is not reduced adequately—or is reduced too abruptly—the risk of hypoglycemia increases. Conversely, maintaining an excessive dose in the immediate postpartum period can also lead to hypoglycemic episodes.

When compared with intermediate‑acting human NPH insulin, insulin glargine has been associated with fewer reported hypoglycemic episodes in clinical studies. Nevertheless, the overall risk remains present, and clinicians must remain vigilant, especially in populations with fluctuating insulin needs such as pregnant or postpartum women.

Animal investigations have demonstrated that insulin glargine can induce severe hypoglycemia, including maternal hypoglycemic shock, confirming the potential seriousness of this adverse effect under certain conditions. These findings underscore the importance of dose accuracy, monitoring, and individualized therapy to prevent hypoglycemia in patients receiving insulin glargine.&lt;SEP&gt;Patients on insulina glargina experienced hypoglycemia rates comparable to those on other regimens.</data>
  <data key="d9">adverse effect,adverse_event,comparable safety,delayed recovery,hypoglycemia,pharmacology,postpartum,reduced_incidence,risk</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-02780a8bc1d372cc47e0ccf2eaab1a99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d0ac8ad5968d54922b924fdb16b61d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61cacf80ada6db26d2b975df763c137a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17844a4a12e6c05ba2d7b5f54b0f9bd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2db4ee43ed7cc39546184320e1328d06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b40c2fbad301807d9646f27d94635cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cd336474a2e9457985162b1ddb4b603&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99841fd6d1e1fbf23f184e202bc760e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecb3968c37f57b78a756e4b661ad089</data>
  <data key="d11">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Basaglar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_Toujeo.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_apidra.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1786315682</data>
