<edge source="Insulin" target="Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d7">15.0</data>
  <data key="d8">(Hypoglycemia, Insulin) denotes the causal relationship in which the administration of insulin can precipitate hypoglycemia, a condition marked by abnormally low blood‑glucose concentrations. The relation is most commonly invoked when the amount of insulin delivered exceeds the metabolic requirements of the patient, whether because of intentional overdosing, miscalculation, or other dosing errors. Excessive insulin delivery—whether as a single superdose or a chronic surplus—has repeatedly been identified as the primary trigger for hypoglycemic episodes in individuals receiving insulin therapy.  

Frequent contributors to insulin‑induced hypoglycemia include giving too much insulin, using a dose that surpasses the patient’s insulin needs, and changes in injection technique or site that alter absorption rates. Such factors can lower glucose levels rapidly and may produce a spectrum of clinical presentations, from mild symptoms such as shakiness and sweating to severe neuroglycopenic events that pose immediate danger to life. Because hypoglycemia is the most frequently reported adverse effect of insulin therapy, clinicians must monitor glucose concentrations closely and be prepared to adjust regimens promptly to maintain glycemic control within a safe therapeutic window, avoiding both hypoglycemia and the opposite extreme of hyperglycemia.  

A minority of the supplied descriptions note that insufficient insulin can, under certain conditions (for example, prolonged fasting, intensive exercise, or concurrent use of other glucose‑lowering agents), also be associated with hypoglycemia. This exception is less common and generally requires additional metabolic stressors; the dominant and well‑established pathway remains excess insulin leading to low blood‑sugar.  

Overall, the (Hypoglycemia, Insulin) relation emphasizes that appropriate insulin dosing, careful patient education, and vigilant glucose monitoring are essential to prevent hypoglycemic events while achieving effective diabetes management.</data>
  <data key="d9">adverse effect,adverse reaction,cause,dosage,dose adjustment,excessive,hypoglycemia,low blood sugar,low_glucose,metabolic risk,overdose,risk,safety,severe reaction,side effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59c9bf7f429a82cbfd7083e36ff8f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4da80e99fd3e43cb7d3b260aca23fe56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb10c00517fa772be2ca9a42f497edf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2943becc796c8ce3f0aaedf0978c3213&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d194d06ef99b3f8aa6b6542b7e93e762&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8934881ec5ab7cfeb836126b3f0e8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d10a1f849386df540104c79e33a7b242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68e2c07786710dd5250c6610f52b14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a6f9e10c0d4af5fff0338eb861f9c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779af610d7298ce98490962cd90b4e76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa6e3b40416ce7934111298208db5346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fe81d733c2a429da0726794b55350b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7715667f5fca178f0988984e932ecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f677e56841fc57bffd8bf5b6cc4a7797</data>
