<edge source="NovoRapid®" target="Fiasp®">
  <data key="d7">21.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fiasp® and NovoRapid® were directly compared in a series of head‑to‑head clinical investigations, representing five of the six studies that examined their use in insulin‑treated diabetes. In at least one 26‑week trial each product was administered together with insulin detemir, providing a consistent background regimen for the efficacy and pharmacokinetic assessments.

Pharmacokinetic profiling shows that Fiasp® is absorbed more rapidly than NovoRapid®. The time to reach 50 % of maximum insulin concentration (t½ Cmax) is about nine minutes shorter, and the overall time to achieve peak concentration (Cmax) is 26 minutes earlier, resulting in a 35‑minute reduction in the interval to 50 % Cmax. During the first 30 minutes after subcutaneous injection, Fiasp® delivers roughly three times more insulin than NovoRapid®, reflecting a higher early exposure that is also observed in pediatric patients. Despite these early differences, total insulin exposure (AUC₀‑₁₂ₕ) and maximum concentration (Cmax) are comparable between the two formulations, and the subcutaneous half‑life of Fiasp® (≈57 min) closely matches that of NovoRapid®.

Efficacy outcomes demonstrate that Fiasp® is at least non‑inferior to NovoRapid® for glycaemic control. In the primary HbA1c endpoint, Fiasp® achieved a treatment difference of –0.17 % with pre‑prandial dosing and +0.13 % with post‑prandial dosing, both meeting the predefined non‑inferiority margin (p &lt; 0.001). Secondary analyses of post‑prandial glucose increment indicate statistically significant advantages for Fiasp®: a –16.79 mg/dL reduction when comparing pre‑prandial values (p &lt; 0.001) and a 7.84 mg/dL improvement for the post‑prandial versus pre‑prandial comparison (p = 0.042). Overall, Fiasp® produced a greater reduction in post‑prandial glucose levels than NovoRapid®.

Safety assessments reveal that the total and maximal hypoglycaemic effects are similar across age groups, indicating comparable risk profiles despite the faster onset of Fiasp®. In elderly patients with type 1 diabetes, Fiasp® produced a larger initial glucose‑lowering effect and higher early insulin exposure while maintaining total exposure and maximum concentration akin to NovoRapid®. These findings suggest that the accelerated absorption of Fiasp® translates into a more rapid glucose‑lowering action without increasing overall hypoglycaemia risk.</data>
  <data key="d9">50% Cmax,AUC,Cmax,clinical study,clinical trial,comparability,comparable half-life,comparative efficacy,comparator,comparison,efficacy,elderly,elderly pharmacokinetics,exposure,faster onset,glucose increase,glucose reduction,greater early exposure,initial hypoglycemic effect,non-inferiority,onset speed,peak concentration,pharmacokinetics,post‑prandial reduction,shorter exposure time,statistical significance,time reduction,total effect,treatment comparison</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2f4f01a72f35d6db3ff333e64f8400ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb9ea85b9ad8cd6940ae41a3ab5d1fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14772c7de5db762c492a75a1c744dae6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff211ed5ca723fa2074871af58a4eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2073bddd90c7dd8e156b67821f0f3247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b97cde53943160602b20f634ba5c1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7ca48868f79f7be782198d67ffefd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7e36686d077dcb6239405f60ea031&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fdc648ec086061141581e27adde6fc4</data>
  <data key="d11">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_fiasp.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1786316199</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
