<edge source="Subcutaneous Insulin" target="Afrezza">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Afrezza is presented as an alternative to subcutaneous insulin with different pharmacokinetics.&lt;SEP&gt;Afrezza requires a higher dose (2:1 conversion) to achieve the same glycemic effect as subcutaneous insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Afrezza has a lower risk of hypoglycemia compared with subcutaneous insulin.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative,comparison,conversion,dosage,safety</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5419de5a030f812ab408ef47f361bebd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8644d98286c4a296063176435b45d589</data>
  <data key="d11">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1786333309</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subcutaneous Insulin" target="Conversion Ratio">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The 2:1 conversion ratio defines how much subcutaneous insulin corresponds to a given Afrezza dose.</data>
  <data key="d9">dosage,equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-8644d98286c4a296063176435b45d589</data>
  <data key="d11">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/edelman-et-al-2026-dosing-afrezza-it-s-not-that-complicated.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1786333351</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
